        action kgRecallIsContentBearing;
        verification kgRecallIsContentBearingDoD : Test { :>> id = "bf3964e2-889b-45aa-8107-8573c5e489d6"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "DONE MEANS: `keel why --brief` and `keel recall` emit a budgeted, ranked, CONTENT-bearing payload fit to inject - compact lines carrying type, name, title, hops, via and source file, plus the substantive text (decision/description/rationale/actionText) of the nearest two - and the JSON form is unchanged for existing callers. Today `seeds` are bare identifiers with no content and the command takes NO flags, so its 40 rows would be noise if injected. THE BUDGET HAS ONE STATED EXCEPTION: the top row is always included even if it alone exceeds the budget, because erasing the most relevant fact is worse than overshooting - every row after the first is inside the budget, footer reserved. VERIFY: at budgets 800/1500/4000 the whole payload FITS; at a budget too small for one row the top row is still present and no contradictory 'nothing reached' is printed; the payload cites a source file per row and carries substance for the top rows; `keel why <term>` JSON keys are unchanged (D0242 part 1)."; }
        // RAN: see sprint478_recall RAN receipt: 14 suites 0 failures + 3 new precision tests; clippy 0; seeds a 11112->431, decision 294->236, gate 5736 correct-unchanged; prompt of common words pushes nothing and names what it ignored; d0239 ranks first with its decision text; keystone(38)+workspace(65) survive; budgets 800/1500/4000 all fit, 200 still shows the top row; .knowledge deleted => recall still works (store is 7 of 13639 items); 641-863ms; why JSON keys unchanged
        part kgRecallIsContentBearingDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "0727b43d-b56a-43ed-a554-a3d5a7e1553f"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "2bec54d"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-27"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action kgRecallSeedsFromAPrompt;
        verification kgRecallSeedsFromAPromptDoD : Test { :>> id = "4db149ec-2962-4f48-a363-748c56eabfda"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "DONE MEANS: `keel recall --prompt -` reads a prompt on stdin and seeds from the CORPUS's own statistics and shape, with ZERO model calls (D0243 supersedes D0242's lexicon-first framing). Four precision rules in preference order: identifier-shaped tokens first (dNNNN, issueNNN, sprintNNN, camelCase element names); word and SEGMENT boundaries, never substrings (camelCase split before matching); rarity weighting DERIVED from the tree, so a token matching thousands of elements is dropped and one matching a handful is a strong seed; and the existing hub suppression. MEASURED BEFORE (substring seeding): decision 294 seeds, change 96, the 795, gate 5806, a 11112 - and 12,893 reached for `a`, which a 40-row cap hides rather than fixes. VERIFY: a prompt of only common words seeds NOTHING and says so; a prompt naming dNNNN or an element name seeds exactly it plus its neighbourhood; `gate` and `the` fall below the rarity threshold while `workspace` and `keystone` survive; recall works with .knowledge/ DELETED; no path spawns a model call."; }
        // RAN: see sprint478_recall RAN receipt: 14 suites 0 failures + 3 new precision tests; clippy 0; seeds a 11112->431, decision 294->236, gate 5736 correct-unchanged; prompt of common words pushes nothing and names what it ignored; d0239 ranks first with its decision text; keystone(38)+workspace(65) survive; budgets 800/1500/4000 all fit, 200 still shows the top row; .knowledge deleted => recall still works (store is 7 of 13639 items); 641-863ms; why JSON keys unchanged
        part kgRecallSeedsFromAPromptDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "e9a1d8c7-cb0a-48a4-956f-2f03d8f3b5f8"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "2bec54d"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-27"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action kgPromptPathInjects;
        verification kgPromptPathInjectsDoD : Test { :>> id = "07258021-4f39-4643-ae17-a22888d9b378"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "DONE MEANS: `keel hook user-prompt` READS the prompt (it took only the root and emitted a fixed routing string), calls recall, and PUSHES the facts ahead of the routing reminder with the VISIBLE count and elapsed time the source process names as this step's produced artifact. Injection pushes whenever an informative token survives; a prompt of only filler or corpus-common words pushes nothing. A CONFIDENCE gate was built here and REMOVED after measurement (2/8 against 5/8 for no gate) - this DoD text is corrected rather than left asserting a gate the code has not had since it was deleted (issue297). THREE KILL SWITCHES, at the human's request, in the order they would be reached for: (1) `keel deactivate knowledge-graph-memory`, the DECLARED off via the existing D0138 unit mechanism - committed, auditable, travels with the project; (2) `KEEL_RECALL=off`, the immediate machine-local off, env-based so it cannot be mistaken for a project decision; (3) a LOW-confidence prompt is simply not injected. Deleting `.knowledge/` is explicitly NOT a kill switch any more - D0243 made seeding corpus-derived - and that is stated because it used to be the removability story. FAIL-OPEN throughout with a hard latency cap that REPORTS rather than hides. VERIFY: a HIGH prompt yields facts with a count and a duration; a LOW prompt and an uninformative one yield the routing reminder unchanged; `keel deactivate` stops injection while `keel why` (pull) still works and reactivating restores it; KEEL_RECALL=off suppresses; the cap forced to 1ms prints a visible SKIPPED note and exits 0; `.knowledge/` deleted leaves the hook injecting and validate + guards green (D0242 part 3)."; }
        // RAN: 14 suites 0 failures, clippy 0; the REAL hook end-to-end: HIGH prompt => '[keel recall - pushed before the model, 809ms]' + d0134 + hooksInBinaryStory + st013 with decision text; LOW ('tidy up the workspace') and uninformative ('change the gate') => routing reminder only; keel deactivate knowledge-graph-memory => 0 recall lines while keel why still works, reactivate => 1 again; KEEL_RECALL=off on the keel process => suppressed; cap forced to 1ms => '[keel recall] SKIPPED - recall took 1002ms (cap 1ms)' exit 0 with routing intact; .knowledge deleted => hook still injects, validate 532 clean, 0 guards failing
        part kgPromptPathInjectsDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "413a360b-8886-46e9-8e5b-7138219f940c"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "2bec54d"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-27"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action kgInjectionProvenOnTheTrap;
        verification kgInjectionProvenOnTheTrapDoD : Test { :>> id = "fc27d485-c3ba-4272-9d09-4aecb997b4c7"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "DONE MEANS: injection is measured against a control on a set the author did not hand-pick, and the result decides whether it ships. MEASURE CORRECTED FROM THE ORIGINAL DoD, which asked for TOOL CALLS TO FIRST CORRECT ANSWER with injection on versus off: I cannot take that measure honestly on myself, because I already know the answers to questions about this corpus, so any tool-call count I produced would measure my memory rather than the retrieval. The mechanical proxy is RETRIEVAL HIT RATE - is the element that answers the request present in the rows the payload actually SHOWS - and the CONTROL is naive keyword search rather than silence, because a model without recall does not sit in the dark, it greps. Scoring the control as zero would have rigged the comparison. QUESTIONS ARE DERIVED, NOT WRITTEN: each query is built mechanically from the target element's BODY text with its own name-words removed, seeded and reproducible (.engine/tools/recall_bench.py), so the set cannot be biased toward what recall happens to find. VERIFY: both arms reported on 50 cases. HONEST OUTCOME CLAUSE: if recall does not beat the control, record that and keep the command tier - do not ship injection because it was planned (D0242 part 4)."; }
        // RAN: 50 mechanically-derived cases, seed 20260828, .engine/tools/recall_bench.py: KG 24/50 (48%) vs naive keyword control 17/50 (34%); overlap both 13, KG-only 11, grep-only 4, neither 22 (union 28/50); KG hit position median 2, top-3 in 14/24; latency mean 1009ms max 1256ms; pushed on 50/50. Earlier 8-case hand-written set with ground truth fixed in advance: 5/8 with no confidence bar vs 2/8 with it, 4 of 5 hits at position 1-2. Injection SHIPS on the strength of beating the control
        part kgInjectionProvenOnTheTrapDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "a6629585-9d06-4013-a774-51a58a5b969b"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "2bec54d"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-28"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action dcPinBites;
        verification dcPinBitesDoD : Test { :>> id = "2783ff89-9608-4043-afbe-a5fca105fa3c"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "Discharges srProjectPinsItsEngine + srWorkspacePinIsCoherent (D0251 clause A/C). The declared engine version becomes BINDING: a write or gate command invoked by a binary whose version differs from engine-version.toml REFUSES naming both versions; read-only views WARN and proceed; version and migrate never refuse (they are how skew is inspected and repaired); an ABSENT declaration stays a warn-only state (D0136: absence is a state - pre-D0190 trees keep working). Workspace: a gate spanning projects whose declarations DISAGREE refuses naming each project and its pin. GHERKIN FIRST, failing before implementation: (1) fixture declares a different version, record decision refuses naming both; (2) same skew, guard refuses; (3) same skew, orient proceeds with a loud warning; (4) migrate proceeds; (5) no declaration, everything proceeds with warn; (6) two-project workspace disagreeing, gate --workspace refuses naming both. VERIFY: all six scenarios green; clippy; full suite; the D0251 escalation warning ships in migrate output so existing projects hear about the change before it bites them."; }
        // RAN: // RAN: keel-cli/tests/pin_actually_bites.rs 6/6 (TDD: written first, run to RED); full workspace suite 18 suites 0 FAILED; discharged by sprint 490
        part dcPinBitesDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "01058d12-9eb4-4033-bfa9-02188d942756"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "5fcaad7"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-29"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action dcKeelWrapper;
        verification dcKeelWrapperDoD : Test { :>> id = "6f9b5365-283a-42be-8ae4-63e50f17adbc"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "Discharges D0251 clause B. A committed POSIX-sh wrapper (keelw) resolves the pinned version from engine-version.toml against a machine-local binary cache (.keel/bin/<version>/), and on a miss downloads THAT VERSION AND NO OTHER from the release page, verifies a committed SHA-256 (keel-wrapper.toml: version -> per-platform sha), and execs. It NEVER falls back to a binary on PATH and never resolves any other version. keel init ships keelw + the toml. TESTABLE WITHOUT THE NETWORK: KEELW_BASE_URL overrides the release origin, so the test serves a fake release from a local directory. GHERKIN FIRST: (1) cache hit execs the cached binary; (2) cache miss + good checksum downloads, caches, execs; (3) BAD checksum refuses loudly and caches nothing; (4) unreachable origin with an empty cache refuses with instructions, never falls back to PATH; (5) the wrapper resolves ONLY the pinned version even when newer assets exist. VERIFY: scenarios green via sh in CI; shipped by init; D0175's supersession-in-part (D0251) already recorded."; }
        // RAN: / RAN: 5 Gherkin scenarios TDD (RED first); discharged by sprint 491
        part dcKeelWrapperDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "fe6860fb-1152-4bd4-9530-a21a06ddafe6"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "b7403e4"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-29"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action dcLibraryReadSide;
        verification dcLibraryReadSideDoD : Test { :>> id = "61dae9e1-aae7-4497-9c5b-2c0f3fe1a02d"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "Discharges srLibraryAvailableWithoutInstall + srAvailabilityIsNotActivation (D0250 clauses A/B/C/F). keel library init <remote> records the library remote in machine-local state beside the console registry; keel library sync is fetch + FAST-FORWARD-ONLY into the machine-local clone (a non-ff is a loud defect report, never a merge); an unreachable remote uses the last-good cache and SAYS how stale it is; keel library list enumerates the units in the cache; keel process import --from-library <unit> imports from the cache exactly as from a directory. GHERKIN FIRST: (1) init + sync clones a local bare fixture; (2) a second sync after upstream commits fast-forwards silently; (3) a doctored local clone (non-ff) reports the defect and refuses to merge; (4) unreachable remote: list still answers from cache and names the staleness; (5) THE DIFFERENTIAL: a project's gate verdict and guard set are byte-identical with the library present and absent - availability is never activation; (6) import --from-library lands a unit identically to directory import. VERIFY: scenarios green; no project tree contains the library; no gate reads it."; }
        // RAN: / RAN: 6 Gherkin scenarios TDD; discharged by sprint 492
        part dcLibraryReadSideDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "5a8bcf8a-8172-40c5-b78f-59518f19ffa6"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "a901722"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-29"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action dcLibraryPublish;
        verification dcLibraryPublishDoD : Test { :>> id = "ea36e5c8-5b74-4b9d-a65b-f7bdeb3b68b6"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "Discharges D0250 clause D. keel process publish <name> exports the unit into the machine-local library clone and COMMITS with a message naming unit + version - always loud, never part of sync. GHERKIN FIRST: (1) publish lands exactly the export file set as one commit in the clone; (2) publishing an unchanged unit refuses as a no-op (issue302 semantics extended to the library: a version that did not move does not publish); (3) publish never pushes without being asked - the push is the human-visible act (or keel land on the library repo). VERIFY: scenarios green; sync remains fast-forward-only after a publish (local commits make the clone ahead, which sync tolerates - AHEAD is not the non-ff defect, BEHIND-divergent is)."; }
        // RAN: / RAN: 3 Gherkin scenarios TDD; discharged by sprint 493
        part dcLibraryPublishDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "3f823664-85cd-4f22-83c5-367a15979bf8"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "34e40b4"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-29"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action dcKickoffReady;
        verification dcKickoffReadyDoD : Test { :>> id = "3261af95-b2ca-4783-9fad-f2c321639c7e"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::demo; :>> procedureText = "The kickoff path, end to end, using everything above. (1) In se-templates: author the exec-summary PROCESS DEFINITION (its skill + template + tests exist; the process file is what makes it a transferable unit - the issue245 class) and publish it to the library. (2) Cut a release tag so a fresh project pins a clean version line. (3) keel init a THROWAWAY project with the shipped keelw, declared profile, first push (issue306 fix proving itself), library sync, import exec-summary + decision-channel from the library, keel onboard read back. VERIFY by demo: the throwaway project's transcript is the evidence - init to first green gate to imported units, no step manual beyond the declared ones. Then the REAL project kicks off on the proven path."; }
