        action recallBenchmarkCanDiscriminate;
        verification recallBenchmarkCanDiscriminateDoD : Test { :>> id = "62250817-726b-43e5-9b41-b23f5f1593e6"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "DONE MEANS: the harness can decide the question it is used to decide. Four defects, all found by the panel and all confirmed. (1) STRAWMAN CONTROL: recall_bench.py builds queries from BODY_FIELDS while baseline() matches only name and title, so the control is structurally denied the one field the queries were sampled from - with bodies included the same control scores 50/50, and real git grep 14/15, both of which are ALSO leakage because the queries are verbatim body words. The harness therefore cannot discriminate in either direction and the 14-point gap is an artifact. (2) NO PRECISION METRIC: recall was measured, precision never was - sampled at ~9% across 17 realistic prompts (1 of 11 pushes topical), which is the metric a PUSH feature lives or dies on. (3) NO SIGNIFICANCE TEST: the 2x2 is KG-hit/grep-hit 13, KG-only 11, grep-only 4, neither 22; McNemar exact on the 15 discordant pairs gives p = 0.119, so the honest reading is 'direction favours KG, parity not excluded', and the net advantage is 7 cases not 24. (4) DEAD STATISTIC: `both` is computed from a name-in-list-of-tuples test that is always false and never printed - evidence the harness was unreviewed while being the sole basis for a delete-the-subsystem proposal. VERIFY: a body-searching control arm; a PARAPHRASE query set not drawn from the target's own text; precision reported beside recall; the 2x2 and McNemar printed; the 548-vs-13704 corpus asymmetry stated; the dead statistic gone."; }
