        action dcRecorderIsDistinctFromJudge;
        verification dcRecorderIsDistinctFromJudgeDoD : Test { :>> id = "a479374a-3962-48aa-b093-f5556560f3cc"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "Resolves issue287. The human accepted d0235 themselves through a human surface while the AI was working, with the note agreed with panel's decisions. delegatedAcceptanceSubstanceRule then FAILED the gate, demanding that the human quote themselves - governance aimed at the one party it must never bind. The rule is right in intent: a DELEGATED acceptance, one an AI records on the human's behalf, must quote their words or cite their gesture, because that is the only thing standing between a real sign-off and a fabricated one. The defect is that NOTHING RECORDS WHO RECORDED an acceptance, only who judged it, so the rule cannot tell a delegated record from a self-recorded one and applies the delegated demand to both. DO: (a) the console's accept path stamps its own channel in the note, appended never substituted, so a self-recorded acceptance self-evidences - the predicate already accepts a cited console or deck gesture; (b) record the RECORDER as provenance distinct from the judge, so the rule can scope itself to genuinely delegated records rather than inferring from note text. VERIFY: an acceptance recorded through the console carries its channel and passes; an AI-delegated acceptance with no quote still FAILS; the human's own words are never altered by either path."; }
