        verification dcSessionActorReachesHooksDoD : Test { :>> id = "63617d31-b425-4072-9847-342de61c62f4"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "Resolves issue337 and CORRECTS issue332. actor::resolve (actor.rs:97-112) is right: explicit, then KEEL_ACTOR, then the machine binding. The ownership guard calls it (guards.rs:2498). The defect is that the Stop hook is spawned by the harness in an environment where KEEL_ACTOR was never set - it is exported per-command in the session's shell - so resolve falls through to the machine binding, which on this machine is a different model than the one working. 22 edits were attributed to the wrong actor and 12 items the session owns were flagged as non-owner edits. DO: a SESSION-scoped binding that resolve reads after KEEL_ACTOR and before the machine binding. The hook payload carries the session id (hook_pre_bash already receives it), so the binding is `.keel/sessions/<id>/actor`, machine-local and gitignored, written by `keel actor set --session <id> <actor>` and by the UserPromptSubmit hook when KEEL_ACTOR is present in its environment. VERIFY: a hook run with no KEEL_ACTOR but a session binding attributes to the session actor; with neither it falls to the machine binding as today; the ownership guard under the hook reports the same count as under the CLI with KEEL_ACTOR set. ARMED: the 12-vs-0 discrepancy observed today reproduces against the current binary. NOT a keystone change: actor.rs and the hook plumbing are outside D0209 clause 2's surface, which is why issue332's stated reason for carrying it was wrong."; }
        // RAN: actor::resolve now reads a per-session binding at .keel/sessions/<CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID>/actor between KEEL_ACTOR and the machine binding; any command run with KEEL_ACTOR remembers it; the hook dispatcher seeds the session id from its payload. LIVE: with KEEL_ACTOR unset - the hook's situation - keel actor resolves to claudeOpus5 (the session) instead of claudeFable5 (the machine), read back rather than assumed. 3 unit tests on the pure halves: read-back by session id, a different session sees nothing, a malformed id never becomes a path, an unchanged declaration does not rewrite. NOT a keystone change - actor.rs and the dispatcher are outside D0209 clause 2 - which is what issue332's carry-reason got wrong
        part dcSessionActorReachesHooksDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "82265feb-9a1f-4cd5-bdc0-61235c0bf5fb"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "24dcc73"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-09-01"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
