        verification dcProcessStepsBindToChecksDoD : Test { :>> id = "d73f9241-c475-46d8-9935-c22a8c07b437"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "Resolves the panel's process-authority finding (rigorProcessAuthorityCritique) and its fact-lifecycle finding (rigorFactLifecycleCritique) - one root: a process is a document with an optional guard, not a machine, because a ProcessStep has no binding to a predicate and everything analytical lives in a String field no control can read. MEASURED: 37 processes, 16 assert any guard, 10 declared human-judgment, 11 UNDECLARED including five authored in the last two days - authoring outruns enforcement and only the author's memory converts one to the other. 'Enforced' means the process asserts a guard SOMEWHERE, not that each step has a check; only agile-workflow's ceremony steps have per-step gates, which is why `keel advance --to` can refuse there and nowhere else. THIS IS A PROPOSAL, NOT A BUILD, because both halves are schema questions under the frozen core: (a) a ProcessStep may declare the guard or rule that checks it - `checkedBy` - so a process can be asked which of its steps are enforced and `advance`-style refusal generalises beyond ceremonies; (b) the analytical fields that controls need to read stop being prose - a Decision's clauses as addressable children, a retro's findings as Issues by construction rather than by grep. DO: write the proposal as a Decision with the two options costed, and put it to the human as a FORK - it changes what a Process IS. VERIFY, for the proposal: it names the 11 undeclared processes and for each says whether a checkedBy binding is possible or whether it joins the 10 declared judgments."; }
