        action dcAutoUpdateVerifiedOrReverted;
        verification dcAutoUpdateVerifiedOrRevertedDoD : Test { :>> id = "f4f0b2ec-1e2d-4adf-801c-f7a5844a58bb"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "Verifies srUpdateIsVerifiedOrReverted and srUnprovenUpdateAsksRatherThanActs. DEPENDS ON dcMigrationRollsBack - without a rollback, verified-or-reverted has no second half. THE GATE IS NOT 'EMPTY PLAN': tested during design and it is vacuous - when the plan is empty migrate returns at migrate.rs:739 BEFORE restamp_pin at :828, so auto-applying an empty plan achieves nothing including not advancing the pin; and any real upgrade plans at least the engine resync, so the criterion never fires where it matters. DO: record the pre-update commit; apply; run the project's own gate (validate + every enforced guard + check-engine); on green retain and report what moved including the pin; on ANY red or interruption restore to the recorded commit and report the gate's verbatim output. VERIFY BOTH OUTCOMES, since a boundary exercised only on the happy path is a claim: a green update is retained AND a red one leaves git status --porcelain EMPTY over .engine and .tracking. The red case is manufactured by shipping a guard the fixture tree violates - the issue089/issue090 class, which is why an empty plan proves nothing about safety."; }
        action dcLibraryStatusShowsDrift;
        verification dcLibraryStatusShowsDriftDoD : Test { :>> id = "1799a4d5-aa8c-4301-bba3-0378cf5ef38b"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "Verifies srDriftIsReportedPerUnit. Today answering 'which of my units are behind' means reading keel library list and keel process show and comparing by hand - the friction the human named when they said they just want better distribution management. DO: one command listing each installed unit with its installed version, the library's version, and the update command for each unit that is behind. VERIFY: a project with a unit deliberately held at an older version reports it BEHIND and prints the command; a project at parity reports none behind; and a project with NO library configured says it cannot tell rather than reporting zero drift - 'nothing is behind' and 'I cannot tell' must not render identically, which is the pass-at-zero hazard this codebase has now caught three times."; }
        // RAN: // RAN: keel status reports units behind AND never-imported, with the exact command for each, plus cache age; an unreachable library reports UNKNOWN rather than zero drift (status_distinguishes_unknown 4/4)
        part dcLibraryStatusShowsDriftDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "5eef6e2f-2c2c-41a2-941f-01ad6049fbbc"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "a16624c"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-31"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action dcScheduledCurrency;
        verification dcScheduledCurrencyDoD : Test { :>> id = "0c41fd98-4461-432c-bd24-bb1e9f673c9e"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "Verifies srUnattendedPullIsIdempotent and closes the unattended half of nUnattendedCurrency. DO: a declared, removable schedule that runs the currency pass for a project - github-pull, library sync, and the drift report - and reports what it found in one place. Autonomy stays bound by D0264: a pull records utterances and NEVER triages, because triage is a judgment and an unattended judgment on untrusted input is the thing the trust tier exists to prevent. VERIFY: two consecutive runs over the same open issues produce identical Statements with no duplicate ids and the second REPORTS its skip count; the pass reports a library it cannot reach rather than treating unreachable as up-to-date; and the schedule is removable by deleting one declared file, per D0163."; }
        action dcUnknownFlagIsRefused;
        verification dcUnknownFlagIsRefusedDoD : Test { :>> id = "e388dac3-cf56-4f7b-b5dc-3539dabe7c27"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "Resolves GH#14. A mistyped or unsupported flag used to be accepted as the ROOT: keel guard --read gated a directory literally named --read, found no files, and reported every guard PASS with 0 scanned. TWO defects compounding - silent argument mis-parsing and pass-at-zero - producing a GREEN RUN OVER NOTHING, which is worse than an error because it is indistinguishable from a clean tree. The same shape recurred while building github-ingest, where a trailing --at value was read as the root, which is why the fix is a SHARED refusal rather than a patch in one command. DO: refuse_flag_as_path on every path-taking entry point (guard, validate, check-engine), naming the argument and saying why refusing beats answering. VERIFY BOTH DIRECTIONS: all three commands exit 2 on --read and emit NO verdict line; and an ordinary ROOT argument still works AND actually scans the corpus - otherwise the refusal would have traded one vacuous pass for another."; }
        // RAN: // RAN: unknown_flag_is_refused 2/2 - guard, validate and check-engine all exit 2 on --read with no verdict line; an ordinary ROOT still scans the corpus (identity-present PASS, non-zero scanned). Reproduced the defect first: every guard PASS, 0 scanned, on a phantom directory
        part dcUnknownFlagIsRefusedDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "c831a583-e72b-4068-ae52-4c33c9f4ab5d"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "9d349dd"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-31"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        // PRIORITY INVERSION HERE IS DELIBERATE (D0258): this resolves a High issue and sits BELOW ten
        // lower-severity items on purpose, because it is BLOCKED ON THE HUMAN. It is a breaking change
        // to a surface 118 call sites invoke by name; ranking unworkable work to the top would make the
        // frontier lie about what can be picked up. It moves the moment they authorise it.
        action dcCliSurfaceConsolidation;
        verification dcCliSurfaceConsolidationDoD : Test { :>> id = "1b4e120f-3aca-4ac5-b3e1-fe47e671e3e4"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "Resolves issue321, and is BLOCKED ON THE HUMAN'S CHOICE - it is a breaking change to a surface other things call by name, so it is not started on my initiative. MEASURED: 111 dispatch arms, ~30 of them read-only lenses performing one act (compute a view, print it); 16 of the 32 declared viewpoints also have a same-named top-level command, and the two mechanisms are only half-connected (keel view orphans fails while keel orphans works); 118 references in .engine skills and processes invoke these commands by name, and guard 39 fails a process naming a tool it did not receive. DO, if authorised: one `keel show <lens>` command routing to the existing implementations, every current name kept as an alias for a deprecation window so nothing breaks on landing day, then the alias list shrinks as call sites move. VERIFY: every lens reachable through the router returns BYTE-IDENTICAL output to its old command (a consolidation that changes an answer is a rewrite wearing a rename's clothes); every alias still works; the visible verb count falls below 25; and guard 39 stays green throughout, since a half-migrated skill reference is exactly what it exists to catch."; }
        // RAN: 35 lens verbs collapsed into keel show <lens>, old names REMOVED with no alias window (the human's choice over my recommendation). BYTE-EQUALITY: 31 of 31 comparable lenses byte-identical against outputs captured from the pre-collapse binary; the 1 that differed was hardening, and the diff WAS the change (it counts dispatched commands, 100 -> 66). 141 call sites rewritten across 57 files in the same commit, so no tree exists where a skill names a command the binary lacks. capability_refusal_bites 5/5 including both inventories held equal to their dispatch and a_retired_lens_verb_is_no_longer_a_command. Workspace suite 463 passed / 0 failed across 36 binaries; clippy clean; all 56 guards pass. MISSED CRITERION, stated: D0273 says the verb count falls below 25 and it is 66 - collapsing the lens family alone does not reach that, and the gate/record/audit families were never measured or argued
        part dcCliSurfaceConsolidationDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "dd22a6e7-6802-4e58-8de5-f253e78e4eb5"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "cf985ec"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-09-01"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action dcObligationDoesNotLockTheTree;
        verification dcObligationDoesNotLockTheTreeDoD : Test { :>> id = "aba588f8-eb0a-494b-b147-025ed2e493d0"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "Resolves issue324. NOT started. Three individually-correct controls compose into a lock: migrate refuses on a dirty tree (migrate.rs:652-687, and its git status --porcelain sees UNTRACKED files), committing to clean it fails because pre-commit validate refuses under engine-version skew (main.rs:226-230, D0251), and skew clears only by running the pinned binary or by migrate. The file that springs it is written by the engine itself - record_obligation (write.rs:471-481) under D0176/K7, whose own doc comment reasons about deadlock at the FILE level and misses it at the TREE level. DO: let migrate proceed when EVERY uncommitted path under .tracking/ is an engine-authored obligation record, and have it NAME the files it tolerated so the exemption is visible rather than silent. Keep the carve-out narrow - .tracking/obligations/ only, untracked-or-added only. The two rejected alternatives are the argument for this one: staging the file does not help (staged is still uncommitted), and degrading validate under skew weakens a gate to work around a different gate. VERIFY: a fixture project that is BEHIND ITS PIN and holds exactly one untracked obligation record runs migrate to completion; the same project with any OTHER uncommitted .tracking file still refuses; and the tolerated path is named in migrate's output. The test must be armed against the current binary, where it deadlocks. NOTE: not reproducible in this repo at all - check_preconditions refuses any tree holding keel-cli/Cargo.toml as a self-build (migrate.rs:664-666) - so this is downstream-only and the fixture is the only way to see it."; }
        // RAN: obligation_does_not_lock_the_tree 4/4 - a project behind its pin holding one engine-written obligation record migrates; the tolerated file is NAMED; any other uncommitted .tracking file still refuses naming what blocked it; a MODIFIED obligation record is not tolerated. ARMED: the escape and naming cases both observed FAILING against the pre-fix binary with the deadlock's own refusal text in the output. The first implementation was correct in principle and did nothing - git collapses a wholly-untracked directory to one entry, so -uall was needed for a per-file decision; found by running it, not by reading it
        part dcObligationDoesNotLockTheTreeDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "d9e4bbd3-f8f8-4531-8d08-076b5fd0d9f5"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "0e001a6"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-09-01"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action dcUnitIdentityIsCarriedNotMinted;
        verification dcUnitIdentityIsCarriedNotMintedDoD : Test { :>> id = "2330649e-16b0-4b19-a1e0-eb018323f01c"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "Resolves issue323. DONE in this commit. THE DEFECT: keel process export resolved a unit's id from unit-ids.toml alone - the registry of ids this project MINTED - so a project that IMPORTED a unit and re-published an improvement found nothing there, minted a fresh id, and shipped the improved content under an identity no consumer had ever installed; next_unit_version then missed on the new id and took its None arm, restarting the version at 1. Content changed, identity changed, version did not, so every consumer's drift check AFFIRMED they were current. DO: make the INSTALL RECORD outrank the minted registry in unit_id_for, repair unit-ids.toml in place when the two disagree (it is what the next export reads), REFUSE when one process name is filed under two ids, and make next_unit_version refuse rather than restart when the id misses on a process the project demonstrably has installed. VERIFY: the round trip issue323 named - publish, import into a second project, change one shipped file, publish again, and assert the unitId is UNCHANGED and the version INCREMENTED - plus the two refusal arms, all three ARMED by running them against the pre-fix binary (D0253) where all three fail. And repair the live damage: exec-summary was filed under two ids in this repo's own install record, both at v1."; }
        // RAN: unit_identity_survives_republish 3/3 - round trip keeps unitId across a republish from an importing project and advances v1->v2 with the improved bytes present; forked identity refuses naming both ids; registry disagreement is repaired in place. ARMED: all 3 FAIL against the pre-fix binary (git stash of process_cmd.rs), the round trip showing the re-mint directly, 9287c762 published over 969856ab. Live repair verified: library exec-summary now unitId a2428932 v2, 151-line SKILL.md, and the duplicate section is gone from installed-units.toml
        part dcUnitIdentityIsCarriedNotMintedDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "b7a8f2c6-2490-432f-9f95-0bc0e308d336"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "93eb6dc"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-09-01"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action dcDodProseCannotBreakTheModel;
        verification dcDodProseCannotBreakTheModelDoD : Test { :>> id = "981f4add-8f9e-461f-b4e6-960f06813b52"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "Resolves issue325. DONE in this commit. THE DEFECT: add_task_locked interpolated its DoD prose RAW into a SysML string literal (write.rs:1193) while every other prose write path bound sanitize_field. A DoD containing a double-quoted phrase closed the literal early and left the BACKLOG UNPARSEABLE, so the next command could not read the model at all - including to report the problem - and the repair had to be made by hand. DONE: sanitize at the WRITE PATH rather than in cmd_add_task, because issue322's lesson was that the previous prose defect recurred from being fixed per-command. SWEPT the whole file first rather than patching the one line: every other interpolation into a SysML literal in write.rs already routes through sanitize_field, so this was the last one. VERIFY: a_dod_containing_quotes_cannot_break_the_backlog writes a DoD carrying a quoted phrase, a newline and a tab, then asserts the interior holds no double quote, the phrase survives as apostrophes, it stays one line, and - the assertion that matters - the file still LEXES AND PARSES. ARMED per D0253 by commenting the sanitize call out: the test fails on the parse assertion."; }
        // RAN: a_dod_containing_quotes_cannot_break_the_backlog 1/1 - a DoD carrying a quoted phrase, a newline and a tab writes an interior with zero double quotes, the phrase surviving as apostrophes, one line, and the file still lexes and parses. ARMED: commenting out the sanitize_field call makes it FAIL at write.rs:1799 on the parse assertion. Swept write.rs first: every other interpolation into a SysML literal already binds sanitize_field, so the count of unsanitized ones is now zero
        part dcDodProseCannotBreakTheModelDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "fc9c06bd-1ffe-4fb4-a7a5-c10570dba346"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "19eb078"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-09-01"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        // CharteredBy d0275: the process Decision states this limit and this task closes it.
        action dcMigrationFixtureIsRealistic;
        verification dcMigrationFixtureIsRealisticDoD : Test { :>> id = "a64f1792-3b69-4af5-9841-4afe638d3812"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "Charters D0275's stated limit. NOT started, and it is the prerequisite that makes every other migration claim more than construction. F9 in federation.rs proves a FRESH SCAFFOLD migrates - refusals fire, pin re-stamped, project comment preserved, adoption survived, gate green - and a fresh scaffold is the EASY case, which is why seven defects got past it. DO: extend the downstream fixture from a scaffold to a REALISTIC project, one that has each of the four properties the seven defects actually came from. (1) It CUSTOMISED an engine-shipped file, so the resync must preserve or conflict rather than silently replace. (2) It authored its OWN skill naming an engine command, so a removed or renamed command is detectable - this is the case D0273's clean break makes urgent. (3) It holds an UNTRACKED obligation record while behind its pin, which is issue324's deadlock and must be reproduced before it can be fixed. (4) It has its own CI workflow, so issue326 and issue327 become visible to a test instead of to a downstream human. VERIFY, and the arming matters more here than usual: cases 3 and 4 must FAIL against today's binary, because they encode defects that are still open - a green suite on all four would mean the fixture is not reproducing them. Cases 1 and 2 assert the behaviour that must hold. The honest completion signal is that the count of defects in this path found by a TEST rather than by a human stops being zero."; }
        // RAN: migration_fixture_is_realistic 3/3 - a lived-in project (behind its pin, a customised engine file, its own skill naming an engine command) migrates; the resync NAMES the files it wrote; the project's own skill survives untouched and the tree gates green; and issue324's escape holds on a realistic project not just a bare one. The fixture found issue328 on its first run - a customised engine file was silently discarded with only a count reported - which is what the fixture exists for. ARMED: emptying report_written makes case 1 FAIL. Case 4 (the project's own CI) deliberately NOT here: issue326/327 are open, so it would assert the defect or be an ignored test; it lands with the fix
        part dcMigrationFixtureIsRealisticDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "5aeb0ad0-98ab-4abf-ae5d-669af2096c2e"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "6c21669"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-09-01"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action dcUnitDeclaresTheCommandsItInvokes;
        verification dcUnitDeclaresTheCommandsItInvokesDoD : Test { :>> id = "1fcaa5d1-aca1-4482-9b5a-d2fce4e924c5"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "Builds D0252 clause A's COMMAND half, unbuilt since the Decision was accepted, and D0273's stated prerequisite. DONE in this commit. THE GAP: clause A promised that a unit declares the capabilities it invokes - guards, schema types AND commands - and that an install whose engine lacks one REFUSES naming the missing capability rather than a version number. Only the guard half shipped (D0183/K8). The token commands appeared nowhere in process_cmd.rs and unit.toml carried skills, rules, guards and extras only, so a renamed or removed command failed SILENTLY downstream: the skill landed and its instructions simply did not run. Clause A even records that renaming a command is a breaking change for units that declare it, recorded so a future rename is not made casually - and the human has now authorised exactly such a rename. DONE: a cli_surface module holding COMMAND_NAMES, the inventory the handshake diffs against; unit_commands derives the commands a unit's own files invoke by scanning for a keel-prefixed token that is ALREADY a dispatched name, so the scan is self-limiting and prose cannot invent a dependency; export writes commands into unit.toml; and the handshake refuses a unit naming a command this binary lacks, before any file is written, with NO --degrade escape - a missing guard leaves prose that still reads correctly, a missing command leaves prose that is actively wrong. VERIFY: the inventory MATCHES the dispatch both ways, parsed from main.rs at test time, which is what makes the const trustworthy since Rust cannot introspect a match at runtime - an inventory that drifts would refuse commands the binary has or accept ones it lacks. Plus: an exported unit declares a real command its text invokes and NOT a non-command token following the same prefix; and importing a unit needing a missing command refuses, names only what is missing, and leaves no file behind."; }
        // RAN: capability_refusal_bites 3/3 - the inventory matches the dispatch BOTH ways parsed from main.rs at test time (it caught one mismatch on first run, --version, a flag spelling not a command); an exported unit declares a real command its text invokes and NOT a non-command token after the same keel prefix; importing a unit needing an absent command refuses, names only the missing one, and leaves no file behind. 99 commands in the inventory. Workspace suite 457 passed / 0 failed; clippy clean
        part dcUnitDeclaresTheCommandsItInvokesDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "1b4c0cb8-da2d-456f-a07d-2ab8b78cc79d"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "21cdce7"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-09-01"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action dcCountAssertionsParseTheNumber;
        verification dcCountAssertionsParseTheNumberDoD : Test { :>> id = "78d08f23-9c8b-4249-b1d1-a25db61a65db"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "Resolves issue329. DONE in this commit. THE DEFECT: two tests asserted a non-zero count by NEGATED SUBSTRING - the form !text.contains('0 <label>') - which is wrong for every count ending in zero, because '580 tracked file(s)' CONTAINS '0 tracked file(s)'. It fired at 370 scanned, was repaired at that one site, and fired again today at 580 tracked files while an IDENTICAL live instance sat in unknown_flag_is_refused.rs untouched by the first repair. DONE: both sites now parse the number via count_before and compare it, which is the only form that says what was meant. AND THE CORRECTION BECOMES A CONTROL (D0047) rather than a third repair: no_test_asserts_a_count_by_substring walks every .rs file under tests/ and fails on any negated-substring count assertion, naming file and line. A lesson is not a control, and the next author reaching for the substring form should be stopped by a failing test rather than by remembering a doc comment. VERIFY: both repaired tests pass; the new control passes over the current corpus and its predicate is narrow enough that prose describing the shape - including its own doc comment - does not trip it. ARMED per D0253 by restoring the OLD form at the status site, where the test then FAILS while the parsed form passes."; }
        // RAN: both sites now parse the count; no_test_asserts_a_count_by_substring walks tests/ and refuses the negated-substring form, naming file and line. status_distinguishes_unknown 4/4 and unknown_flag_is_refused 3/3. ARMED: restoring the OLD substring form at the status site makes it FAIL where the parsed form passes
        part dcCountAssertionsParseTheNumberDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "b07f9fc8-7070-451c-a92e-1ef000a96345"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "6c21669"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-09-01"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action dcTurnGateFailsClosed;
        verification dcTurnGateFailsClosedDoD : Test { :>> id = "bbe7ef86-738b-4112-b32d-435b8edcb404"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "Resolves the panel's Critical finding (rigorTopologyCritique). The Stop hook - the turn gate - is fail-OPEN: on its own error it prints a warning and ALLOWS the turn to end. So do post-edit and subagent-stop. Only pre-write and the pre-commit git hook fail closed. A gate whose error path permits is a suggestion with a green light attached, and it holds exactly when nothing goes wrong - which is not when a rogue turn happens. DO: the Stop hook's ERROR path (validate crashed, a guard panicked, the binary could not be read) DENIES with the error text, the same way pre-commit does; post-edit stays advisory because a per-edit deny would trap editing on transient states, but says plainly that it is advisory. The harness TIMEOUT resolving to allow cannot be changed from inside keel and stays a recorded residual visible in the fire-ledger. VERIFY: a fixture where validate returns an error (not a violation - an ERROR) makes hook stop exit non-zero with the error in its output; a green tree still returns 0 silently; and the hardening enforcementPoints table reports Stop as fail-closed on error. ARMED against the current binary where the error path allows."; }
        // RAN: the Stop hook's wrapper now emits a block decision when the keel binary is not found, instead of printing 'this gate did not run' and allowing; advisory hooks keep D0134's fail-open. Test asserts both: the Stop command contains decision:block and the UserPromptSubmit command does not. Live: .claude/settings.json regenerated by sync-claude and read back BLOCKING; keel hardening enforcementPoints reports 3 fail-closed of 12 (Stop, pre-write, pre-commit), up from 2. The in-function error paths (validate error, rule-eval error, guard error) were already denied and are now described truthfully in the table. Residual: the second red pass still yields by design, and the harness timeout still allows
        part dcTurnGateFailsClosedDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "64ad4969-cccf-4d2a-8922-e026c1568990"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "a52d81b"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-09-01"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action dcRetroFindingsRoute;
        verification dcRetroFindingsRouteDoD : Test { :>> id = "22371286-ce2b-4e97-b38b-d518abb60419"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "Resolves the retro_backlog control defect (rigorSubstanceCritique, registered under D0278). THE PREDICATE, at guards.rs:1601-1628: fires only if the retro text contains the literal tokens AVOIDABLE-ISSUE or LESSON:, then is satisfied by names_tracked_item, which returns true when ANY dc-prefixed name appears anywhere in the file. Every sprint file names the task it delivered, so the check is satisfied by construction; and a retro that says FINDING is never examined. Today three control defects and three other findings went through it unexamined. The `changed` parameter - the staged file list - is accepted and discarded (`let _ = changed`), which is the tell: the guard was designed to ask whether THIS COMMIT records a new tracked item and never did. DO: (1) drop the vocabulary gate - a retro gate section with method analyze IS a findings record, examine all of them; (2) the satisfying condition is that the commit ADDS a tracked item - a new `part issueNNN` or `action dc...` in the staged diff - or the retro carries one of the explicit no-item justifications; naming an existing task is not enough, because that is what every retro does; (3) a violation, not a warning, once the corpus is clean. VERIFY: a retro whose analyze text contains a finding and whose commit adds no Issue or task FAILS; the same commit with a `record issue` in it passes; an explicit 'no new item: already tracked as issueNNN' passes. ARMED: today's sprint507 retro text with no new item must fail against the fixed predicate and passes against the current one."; }
        // RAN: retro_backlog on its third shape: every analyze-method retro gate examined regardless of vocabulary; satisfied only when THIS COMMIT adds a tracked item the retro's own text names, or an explicit no-item justification; a violation not a warning. 8 unit cases pass including both earlier failure modes as regressions - the co-staged-file hole (issue189) and the names-its-delivered-task hole (issue335) both FAIL against the new predicate. names_tracked_item and the vocabulary marker list are deleted. Live: this commit's own retro (sprint 514) names issue334-337, which this commit adds, and passes
        part dcRetroFindingsRouteDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "cb2eca0c-7c78-42ca-98de-ecaf59b6f325"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "a52d81b"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-09-01"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action dcRemainingCliFamilies;
        verification dcRemainingCliFamiliesDoD : Test { :>> id = "6bd11590-5cb4-4dfc-8867-4ff488b97327"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "Resolves issue338. NOT started. D0273's consequences promise the visible verb count falls BELOW 25. The lens collapse yields 66 - 100 dispatch arms minus 35 lens verbs plus one router. The criterion was written without deriving it from the measurement in the same Decision's context (43 lenses of ~111 arms already implied roughly 66), so it is a wish with a number attached, and the work would have been called done against it. TWO HONEST RESOLUTIONS, and this task exists to make one of them happen rather than let the miss stand as a recorded criterion nobody revisits. (a) Collapse the remaining families the same way: gate (12 - validate, guard, gate, check, check-engine, rules, assured, reverify, 4 audits), record (11), and the orient/surface/github set (17). Each is a measured consolidation with its own byte-equality baseline, and each is BREAKING with call-site rewrites landing in the same commit via a D0067 transform - the shape sprint 512 established. (b) Amend D0273 by a superseding Decision that states the achieved number and withdraws the 25. DO: measure the remaining families first - how many are read-only lenses in disguise versus verbs that earn their name - and put the choice to the human as a fork with the counts, because (a) is a second breaking change to a surface other things call by name and is not started on my initiative. VERIFY, for whichever is chosen: `keel hardening` helpCoverage.dispatched reads back the number the Decision states, and the two agree."; }
        action dcTestsDoNotWriteTheLiveTree;
        verification dcTestsDoNotWriteTheLiveTreeDoD : Test { :>> id = "8327a0eb-72ed-43c4-8912-e88939c46374"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "Resolves issue339. DONE in this commit. adoption_check_can_fail.rs ran `keel process export intake` with no cwd, so the export resolved to the LIVE repository root - and export writes the origin's install record (D0183), so every test run bumped the real project's intake unit version in .engine/contracts/installed-units.toml. Found because the file showed as modified AFTER `git add -A`, during the suite. DONE: the export runs from the test's own scaffolded fixture, which has the process because init copies .engine. VERIFY: the test passes, and after a full run the live installed-units.toml is unmodified - checked with git status, not assumed."; }
        // RAN: adoption_check_can_fail 2/2 with export running from its own fixture; after the run, git status shows installed-units.toml unmodified (0 lines), where before the fix it showed the intake version bumped
        part dcTestsDoNotWriteTheLiveTreeDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "dd32f941-0b9b-45c5-954f-34f64351c9b6"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "3bb0125"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-09-01"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action dcPublishSaysWhatMoved;
        verification dcPublishSaysWhatMovedDoD : Test { :>> id = "fcd7bffb-507a-49b2-a5e5-4bc7370dd657"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "Resolves issue340. NOT started, Low. publish's no-op detection (issue302) compares the unit's FILE hashes against the install record and holds the version when they match - correct. But it does not compare the MANIFEST, so a manifest-only change (today: every unit gained a `commands` line under D0252 clause A) produces a library commit titled 'publish X vN' at an unchanged N, and the log reads as if the same version was published twice. DO: the no-op check hashes the manifest it would write against the manifest in the library clone; identical files AND identical manifest is 'nothing to publish'; changed manifest with unchanged files commits with a message that says so - 'publish X vN (manifest only)'. VERIFY: re-running publish with no change is a stated no-op and no commit; a manifest-only change commits once with the qualified message and a second run is a no-op."; }
        verification dcExternalFactGateDoD : Test { :>> id = "ca28a415-669d-4425-b30b-b84c8cf1d833"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "DONE MEANS: one class of TestResult can be satisfied ONLY by an external fact keel queries, never by the agent's assertion. Landscape finding (Beads gh:run gates): a gate that closes only when a real green CI run exists for the judged SHA is a gate the agent cannot talk its way past; keel's append-gate-result --evidence still records what the agent SAYS it ran. DO: a method or evidence kind - ci-run - whose TestResult carries the GitHub run id and workflow name; CI (not the agent) verifies on every push that each ci-run result's run id exists, is for the same repository, concluded success, and its head SHA equals the result's judgedAgainst; a mismatch is a violation, a result the agent wrote with a fabricated id is caught by the party the agent cannot write to. Beads' second-order lesson applies: bd gate check is a command the agent runs; keel's check runs in CI. VERIFY: a fixture result naming a real green run passes; a fabricated run id fails naming the result; a real run for a DIFFERENT sha fails; the check is in ci.yml under the keystone lock."; }
        // RAN: ci-run id=33978282844 workflow=ci (the CI run for 50175b68d8fb95f333b6d66c7806c580ab3d42b2, success) - and the same suite: 521/0 exit 0 incl. ci_runs unit tests; keel audit-ci-runs . reads this receipt
        part dcExternalFactGateDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "78204709-bb91-442e-af7b-dce4ea66a705"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "50175b68d8fb95f333b6d66c7806c580ab3d42b2"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-09-05"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action dcSkillsRouteBehaviourally;
        verification dcSkillsRouteBehaviourallyDoD : Test { :>> id = "0be54ddd-4d7a-4d4f-a4a6-f9ec71494b42"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "DONE MEANS: whether a skill actually routes the agent is TESTED, not assumed. Landscape finding (Superpowers tests/claude-code rig, 280k stars and zero enforcement - but the one thing it has that keel lacks): keel has 57 guards over the model and no test over whether its ProcessStep prose and skill descriptions cause the agent to invoke the process. This is the tractable half of the process-authority finding (dcProcessStepsBindToChecks) that needs no schema change: test the prose behaviourally. DO: a harness that runs a real model against a fixed set of prompts, one per process the project has activated, and asserts from the transcript that the intended skill was invoked before any write - reported as a coverage number per skill (routed / not routed / ambiguous), run on a schedule not per commit because it costs tokens, and its number tracked as an indicator (D0088) until a defensible threshold exists. VERIFY: the rig runs against the current skill set and reports a number; at least one skill measured as NOT routing is recorded as an Issue with the prompt that missed it."; }
        action dcCommitDeltaView;
        verification dcCommitDeltaViewDoD : Test { :>> id = "eb2078c6-1687-4659-a43a-19e85c7303ff"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::demo; :>> procedureText = "DONE MEANS: the delta a commit makes to Needs, Requirements, Decisions and Issues is rendered where the human's authority already lives, before they read a line of code. Landscape finding (OpenSpec team-workflow): a reviewer given the delta spec ahead of the diff sees WHAT the change is supposed to do; keel's commits arrive at GitHub as SysML hunks. keel commits to main without PRs, so the surface is the decision-channel issue body and the exec-summary page. DO: a computed #View - commit-delta - over a git range that lists items added, superseded and resolved by type, with their titles, and renders as the top of the decision-channel comment for the Decisions in that range and as a section on the published decision page (decision-surfacing). Never stored. VERIFY: for the last landed range the view names every new Decision, Issue and task in it, reconciled against git diff by count; the channel comment carries it; a range with no model change renders empty rather than inventing prose."; }
        action dcDeliberateRankIsRecorded;
        verification dcDeliberateRankIsRecordedDoD : Test { :>> id = "38e7778d-b9ff-4453-9f2e-5756fbda1f90"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "DONE MEANS: a deliberate priority inversion can be RECORDED and the guard honours the record. Today priority-inversion warns 'if that is deliberate say so' and reads nothing anyone could say it in - a control asking for prose with no home, which is the manual-vigilance shape D0047 rejects. MEASURED 2026-09-02: ranking the human's stated first step (st066, the computed control structure and its CliCommand prerequisite) at the head of the frontier produced 30 warnings that no fact can discharge, although the reason is a recorded Statement. DO: a typed edge - #PrioritizedBy dependency from the item to the Statement or Decision that ranks it - suppresses the warning for that item and prints the citation instead; an item ranked above High work with no such edge still warns. VERIFY: the two items above resolve to zero warnings once the edge to st066 is authored; removing the edge restores the warning; an edge to a non-Statement non-Decision is a violation."; }
        action dcUnknownMarkerRefused;
        verification dcUnknownMarkerRefusedDoD : Test { :>> id = "2af58583-2649-48bb-baaf-fc377d119590"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "DONE MEANS: record decision --from refuses an unrecognised marker value by name and writes nothing. MEASURED 2026-09-02: marker: prospective-change - a plausible spelling of the real process-change - produced an UNMARKED Decision silently (D0282 landed that way; D0283 nearly did), and the locked-file edit the Decision was meant to authorise was refused by process-change for want of the marker that had been written. The vocabulary is two words (process-change -> #ProspectiveChange, safety-change -> #SafetyChange). VERIFY: the integration test an_unknown_decision_marker_is_refused_not_dropped - exit 2, the value named in the refusal, the decisions directory unchanged."; }
        // RAN: cargo test -p keel-cli --test unknown_flag_is_refused an_unknown_decision_marker_is_refused_not_dropped 1/1: exit 2, refusal names prospective-change, .engine/decisions count unchanged; the live D0283 file was marked by hand after the silent drop was observed
