        action dcResponseContractGate;
        verification dcResponseContractGateDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d650001-0813-4501-9b3e-000000150001"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::demo; :>> procedureText = "srDc response contract + Tier-3 in-loop gate (D0130). WHY: the response contract (parse-first routing, no prose state, no fabricated attestation) lived ONLY in CLAUDE.md — injected as ordinary user-turn context, the weakest layer that can hold a rule — with nothing checking it; sprint 247/248 recorded four bypasses of ALREADY-ACCEPTED decisions (issue082-085). DELIVERED: (a) `.claude/output-styles/keel.md` + `outputStyle: keel` — the contract now sits in the SYSTEM PROMPT, and is the CANONICAL home (CLAUDE.md references it, never restates it, so the two cannot drift into dual truth per D0105); (b) the `Stop` hook wired in `.claude/settings.json` running .engine/tools/stop_gate.py, which executes keel validate + guard at the TURN boundary and BLOCKS the turn while the model is dishonest, feeding violations back — harness-executed, so it ENFORCES rather than instructs; (c) two real defects fixed in the prototype while productionising it: it hardcoded a Windows-only `keel.exe` and so would have silently NO-OPPED on every other contributor's machine (the issue076 class inside the governance control itself), and it decoded subprocess output with the platform default so its feedback arrived as mojibake. VERIFIED by pipe-test: green tree -> exit 0, no output (stop allowed); injected parse error -> {\"decision\":\"block\"} with the violations in `reason`; stop_hook_active=true + still red -> allows the stop with a loud systemMessage (loop-safe, never traps the agent); missing binary -> visible systemMessage, never a silent no-op; encoding verified at byte level (em-dash U+2014 present, no U+FFFD, no cp1252 mojibake) rather than by reading the console. settings.json validated: JSON parses, outputStyle=keel, Stop command+timeout resolve, and the pre-existing 10 permission denies + 2 UserPromptSubmit hooks are PRESERVED. NOT DELIVERED (stays on keelInLoopGates): the D0128 measurement, Tier-2 PostToolUse per-edit gating, Tier-1 PreToolUse deny. NOT VERIFIABLE BY THE AI: that the Stop hook actually fires — the harness must reload settings (`/hooks` or restart), and a hook cannot be proven to fire within the turn that wires it."; }
        part dcResponseContractGateDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "2a1ec890-7051-456b-a644-0c3fbae37797"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "7740eb0"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-13"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action dcDeclaredMarkers;
        verification dcDeclaredMarkersDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d5e0006-0812-4e06-9b3e-00000000e006"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "srDcDeclaredMarkerVocabulary (D0129). Of the markers guards consume, ONLY #View is declared: #Capability (15 files), #DerivedFrom (22), #Covers (6), #Dispositions (10), #ProcessDefect (5) appear nowhere as `metadata def`, yet #DerivedFrom is load-bearing for the hard-blocking requirement-rootedness guard (guards.rs:386-398, view.rs:3085-3116). Markers are not type-checked, so a MISSPELLED marker validates clean and silently removes that item from the guard's view — a blind guard reports PASS, and the exposure scales with contributor count. DO: declare the missing markers in .engine/schema/core/relationships.sysml and make validation REJECT an undeclared/misspelled marker. NOTE schema/core is FROZEN: this needs explicit human sign-off before the schema edit. VERIFY: all guard-consumed markers are declared; a deliberately misspelled marker FAILS validation (today it passes); requirement-rootedness still sees every #Capability item it saw before (no regression in scanned counts); clippy + cargo test + validate + guard + check-engine green."; }
