        action dcRuleAbsenceNotViolation;
        verification dcRuleAbsenceNotViolationDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d7a0001-0814-5a01-9b3e-0000002a0001"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "Resolves issue090 — the SECOND instance of D0136's class, found by deliberately hunting for more after issue089 rather than assuming it was isolated. Six HARD guards are rule-sourced (charter, issues, requirement-rootedness, decision-rationale, acceptance-events, confirmation-authenticity) and a MISSING rule made rule_violations return Err, which each guard reported as a VIOLATION. Reproduced on a fresh `keel init` project with `.engine/rules/` removed: SIX hard failures, exit 1, every commit blocked, with messages naming internal rule names (acceptanceEventRule, charterRule, ...) that mean nothing to a downstream project. Any project whose .engine/rules/ is absent, older, or authored fresh per D0093 would hit this. FIX: `view::rule_violations_opt` returns Ok(None) for an ABSENT rule; each guard maps that to a WARNING ('this control is NOT ADOPTED by this project') plus a pass — never silent, so deleting a rule to dodge a gate stays visible — while a MALFORMED rule still fails via Err. A project that never adopted a control has not violated it. VERIFY (all measured): fresh project minus .engine/rules -> 6 failures become ALL PASS exit 0 with 6 visible NOT-ADOPTED warnings; fresh project WITH rules still passes; this repo unaffected; NEGATIVE TEST — an injected untriaged Issue with issuesTriagedRule present still hard-fails with exit 1, so the protection is intact. clippy + cargo test + keel validate + guard green."; }
        part dcRuleAbsenceNotViolationDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "30728921-a0c6-488f-9937-e333971af015"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "b98f526"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-14"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action dcVersionIdentification;
        verification dcVersionIdentificationDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d7e0001-0814-5e01-9b3e-0000002e0001"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "Resolves issue091. Three versioned releases (v0.2.0/0.2.1/0.2.2) shipped with NO version identification in the artifact: `--version`, `-V` and `version` all printed usage. A guard's behaviour is a property of the BINARY, so 'which binary are you running' is the first diagnostic step for every blocked-downstream report (issue089, issue090) — and it was answerable by neither the reporter nor the maintainer, leaving a project still on the blocked version INDISTINGUISHABLE from one that had upgraded. DO: `keel version` (also --version / -V / --json) reporting the release version, the BUILD COMMIT baked by a new keel-cli/build.rs, and the guard inventory computed from GUARD_NAMES rather than restated. The commit is required because the version string alone cannot separate the published artifact from a stale local build claiming the same number; it degrades honestly (`unknown` off-git, `+dirty` from a modified tree) rather than guessing, and is checkable against the recorded Release facts in baselines.sysml. VERIFY (all measured at b006f10): all four invocation forms print version + commit + guard split, exit 0; `--json` emits the same as machine-readable; it answers from OUTSIDE a keel project (a caller establishing which binary they hold may be standing anywhere), so it resolves before any root resolution; `+dirty` correctly flagged a modified tree; a unit test asserts WARNING_ONLY_GUARDS is a strict subset of GUARD_NAMES, since a stray name would make the reported hard-blocking count overstate enforcement — the same defect class as the version gap itself, a number a reader trusts that nothing checks. clippy clean; 157 tests; validate + all 23 guards + check-engine green."; }
        part dcVersionIdentificationDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "0d7e0002-0814-5e02-9b3e-0000002e0002"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "b006f10"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-14"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action dcHookConfigIntegrity;
        verification dcHookConfigIntegrityDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d800001-0814-6001-9b3e-000000300001"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "Resolves issue093, reported by the human ('why are the stop hooks breaking?'). D0134 deleted .engine/tools/stop_gate.py when the in-loop gates moved into the binary, and .claude/settings.json got the replacement — but .claude/settings.local.json ALSO declared a Stop hook pointing at the deleted script, and Claude Code MERGES hooks across settings files, so both fired and the stale one failed on EVERY turn end for a full sitting. It survived because settings.local.json is GITIGNORED (.gitignore:70): invisible to git status, to every diff, and to the doc-sync grep, which sweeps the tracked surface. FIX: remove the stale hook, and add the `hook-config-integrity` guard checking that every hook `command` in .claude/settings*.json naming a repo-relative script actually exists. WARNING-level on purpose — the config is machine-local and partly gitignored, so CI cannot see it and one contributor's personal hook must never block another's commit; a warning still fires on every `keel guard`, including inside the Stop hook, making a sibling hook's breakage self-reporting. VERIFY (all measured): the real failure reproduced (`can't open file ... stop_gate.py`) before the fix; guard clean at 3 scanned after removal; NEGATIVE TEST — restoring the exact stale settings.local.json produces the warning naming the missing script, and removing it returns to clean; absolute paths and .exe references are deliberately not flagged (an unbuilt binary is a legitimate transient state the hook commands already probe for); a malformed settings JSON is reported, since every hook it declares is silently inert. `keel version` reports 24 guards (18 hard, 6 warning-only), computed from GUARD_NAMES. Doc-sync: guards.md catalogue + count, CLAUDE.md §5. clippy clean; 157 tests; validate + all 24 guards + check-engine green."; }
        part dcHookConfigIntegrityDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "0d800002-0814-6002-9b3e-000000300002"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "97530bf"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-14"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action dcSupersededNotReady;
        verification dcSupersededNotReadyDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d80003f-0814-603f-9b3e-00000030003f"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "Resolves issue100. `ready` was computed from successions and done-state only; `#Supersede` was consumed in view/guards/serve/write but referenced NOWHERE in orient.rs or indexer.rs, so a supersede edge had no effect on the frontier. Since the frontier is auto-followed (D0052), two items D0140 had just forbidden — whose execution would have silently deleted 493 edges — remained scheduled for the next session. FIX: `view::superseded_names` returns `#Supersede` edge targets; `ready` excludes them, conservative on error so a model-read failure cannot silently restore everything to ready. VERIFY (measured): ready 27 -> 25 with both superseded items gone; `outstanding` unchanged at 27, because a retired item is NOT done and reporting it as done would be a lie; a non-superseded ready item is unaffected; clippy clean; 161 tests; validate + all 25 guards + check-engine green."; }
        part dcSupersededNotReadyDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "0d800040-0814-6040-9b3e-000000300040"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "e338eae"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-14"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        // Human-raised 2026-08-14, mid-pass: "while building, we'll need a migration tool to get
        // projects leveraging keel up-to-date." Ranked with the conversion cohort, not after it — a
        // migration path retrofitted once ten conversions have landed cannot reconstruct what each one
        // changed, and every conversion in this pass widens the gap for downstream projects.
        action dcSchemaSweepResidue;
        verification dcSchemaSweepResidueDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d80014b-0814-614b-9b3e-00000030014b"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::inspect; :>> procedureText = "Resolves issue108. D0142's leanness sweep deleted `Risk`, `Mitigation` and `DesignElement` and left four pieces of residue behind: `.engine/schema/core/risk.sysml` is hollow (its header and doc string still describe the deleted types; its only content is `enum def RiskStatus`, which nothing references); `RiskLevel` in `element.sysml` is orphaned the same way, its only consumers having been `Risk`'s three level attributes; `architecture.sysml`'s header comment still lists `DesignElement`; and `sysmlv2-syntax-notes.md` still lists `EngineRisk` among the packages to import. DO: delete `risk.sysml` and `RiskLevel`, and correct both docs. VERIFY: no `EngineRisk`, `RiskStatus`, `RiskLevel` or `DesignElement` reference survives outside `.engine/decisions/` (where the historical record must stay intact); the dead-schema rule reports nothing new; validate + all guards + check-engine green; the kernel is run on the change, since the last two schema deletions in this pass each broke a reference the Rust validator accepted. NOTE: this is a `schema/core` change and therefore a CHANGE route needing its own Decision and sign-off, which is why it was NOT bundled into sprint 281. Deleting `risk.sysml` from the engine does not strand a downstream project: `keel migrate` never deletes a file it does not ship, so the project keeps its copy and the resync reports it as unrecognised."; }
        part dcSchemaSweepResidueDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "0d80019b-0814-619b-9b3e-00000030019b"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "b745cc2"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-15"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action dcProjectMigrationTool;
        verification dcProjectMigrationToolDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d80003e-0814-603e-9b3e-00000030003e"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::demo; :>> procedureText = "A downstream project's `.engine/` is a COPY taken at `keel init` time, so it diverges from the binary the moment the engine changes — and this pass changes the schema repeatedly. That divergence has already blocked two projects entirely (issue089: 566 violations, every commit blocked; issue090: six hard guards failing), and both were fixed by making the BINARY tolerant, which works for a vocabulary set and does not work for a schema conversion: no amount of binary tolerance retypes a project's `part def Process` into an `action def`. DO: `keel migrate [--dry-run]` — a versioned, ordered set of migration steps, each idempotent, that brings a project's on-disk `.engine/` and `.tracking/` from any prior vintage to the current one. NON-NEGOTIABLES, from D0067 and from what the field reports already cost: a DRY RUN that reconciles control totals per step and refuses on any mismatch; idempotent steps, so a re-run is a no-op rather than a double-application; NEVER fabricate history — a migration rewrites authored facts only where the transform is mechanical and total, and reports anything it cannot transform rather than guessing; it must REFUSE on a dirty tree, since a half-migrated working copy mixed with uncommitted human edits is unrecoverable; and it must state the vintage it detected and the steps it will run BEFORE running them. Each conversion sprint in this pass CONTRIBUTES its migration step as part of its own DoD — the step is written by whoever makes the change, when they still know what it changed, not reconstructed later. VERIFY: a project pinned at the pre-pass engine vintage migrates to current and then passes its OWN full gate (validate + all guards + check-engine) — the only test that matters, since that is the state a downstream user is in; a second run changes nothing; a dry run on that project reports every step and reconciles totals; a project already current is a no-op reporting so; a dirty tree is REFUSED with the reason; a project that authored its own `.engine` content fresh per D0093 is migrated without clobbering that content; the vintage is DETECTED rather than asked for. Also verify the failure path honestly: a step that cannot transform an item reports the item and exits non-zero rather than leaving a partial migration silently."; }
        part dcProjectMigrationToolDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "0d80014a-0814-614a-9b3e-00000030014a"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "8b785ff"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-14"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        // PANEL OUTCOME (2026-08-14): the three critics reached unanimity that D0139(E) must not be
        // executed, by three independent routes. These four items carry that outcome. dcVerificationLink
        // ranks first because an accepted Decision currently instructs a migration that would silently
        // destroy the verification spine.
        action dcVerificationLinkDecision;
        verification dcVerificationLinkDecisionDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d80003a-0814-603a-9b3e-00000030003a"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::inspect; :>> procedureText = "Resolves issue098. `objective r;` DECLARES a nested member and carries NO edge — kernel-verified with a deliberately undeclared name, against controls that correctly fail. So the shape recorded since June as 'the only valid verification shape' is not a relationship, and D0139(E) instructs a migration onto it that would leave every verification link silently non-existent while both toolchains report clean. DO: author the superseding Decision (D0140, PROPOSED — supersedes D0139 clauses E and G only, leaving the base-first rule intact) recording that #Verify and #DerivedFrom are RETAINED with kernel-verified justification: for #Verify, 309 of 456 edges target `part Decision`, which cannot be a requirement objective, and the only reference-bearing forms (`objective :>> r` / `:> r`) require a requirement-typed target; for #DerivedFrom, all 37 edges are cross-metaclass and D0055 already records that cross-metaclass `:>` is impossible. This is the base-first rule WORKING, not failing — D0139(B) demands a recorded justification that no base construct suffices, and these two now have one. VERIFY: D0140 supersedes only E and G, with the #ProspectiveChange marker and the D0070 keystone co-commit; relationships.sysml:7-12 is corrected so it no longer presents the objective shape as an edge; sysmlv2-syntax-notes.md gains the declares-vs-references distinction with the discriminator method; the two migration backlog items are superseded rather than left instructing a harmful change; validate + guard + check-engine green."; }
        part dcVerificationLinkDecisionDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "0d800041-0814-6041-9b3e-000000300041"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "e338eae"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-14"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action dcMarkerCensusView;
        verification dcMarkerCensusViewDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d80003b-0814-603b-9b3e-00000030003b"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "Resolves issue099. I recorded #Verify at 613, #DerivedFrom at 91 and #DependsOn at 91 in an accepted Decision; the real EDGE counts are 456, 37 and 42. The inflation came from grepping the marker name, which also matches the 129 prose mentions inside `description`/`procedureText` strings — a corpus that contains prose about itself inflates its own census, and it grows as more is written about the migration. guards.rs:936 and relationships.sysml:66 already carried the correct 456. DO: compute the census instead of grepping it — a marker-census view or `keel` subcommand counting EDGE STATEMENTS per marker (and reporting prose mentions separately, since the gap between the two is the tell), so every future migration quotes one place. Correct the recorded totals in D0139/issue095 via the superseding Decision rather than editing an accepted record. VERIFY: the census reports 456/37/42 for the three markers and agrees with guards.rs; prose mentions are reported separately and are non-zero; a marker with zero edges is reported as zero rather than omitted (#SafetyChange is exactly this case); the figure is reachable from a declared view; clippy + cargo test + validate + guard green."; }
