        action dcPanelCritiqueRecords;
        verification dcPanelCritiqueRecordsDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d80003c-0814-603c-9b3e-00000030003c"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::demo; :>> procedureText = "The human commissioned a three-critic panel (antagonist / protagonist / dispassionate technical-correctness) and asked for iterative deliberation to unanimity. The panel RAN and reached unanimity on the central question, but its findings currently exist only in conversation history — which D0018 forbids as a home for anything a successor needs. DO: author the panel's findings as engine-native critique facts (method=critique, `#Verify`-linked to the critiqued element, with `lens` and `severity`), each attributed to a registered critic actor. NOTE THE INTEGRITY CONSTRAINT and resolve it explicitly rather than by convenience: `critic-independence` requires judgedBy != the element's author, and these critics were separate agent CONTEXTS but the same model as the author. Registering them as actors is honest only if the actor record states exactly that — a distinct critique context, not a distinct model — so a later reader cannot mistake them for independent reviewers. Decide and record whether that satisfies critic-independence or merely passes it; if it merely passes it, say so in the record. VERIFY: every finding the panel carried into a tracked item has a critique record citing its evidence; the critic actors' records state what they actually were; critic-independence passes for a stated reason rather than by accident; no finding is recorded as unanimous that was not; validate + guard green."; }
        part dcPanelCritiqueRecordsDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "0d80015b-0814-615b-9b3e-00000030015b"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "55f181e"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-14"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action dcSchemaLeannessSweep;
        verification dcSchemaLeannessSweepDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d80003d-0814-603d-9b3e-00000030003d"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::demo; :>> procedureText = "The panel's non-marker findings, which all three critics considered more consequential than the marker count and which the human's phase-1 ask (lean, fact-laden items) targets directly. Each needs its own measurement before action; this item is the CHARTER for that sweep, not a licence to change schema. (a) TWO live `Release` types — `part def` (work.sysml:63, 0 instances, carries a dead `membershipRule`) and `occurrence def` (baseline.sysml:18, 11 instances) — and deploy.sysml:6 imports the 0-instance one, threading it through four action signatures. (b) ~15 never-instantiated types persist in frozen schema/core while rules.sysml:63-66 records the OPPOSITE policy for `OrderingRule` ('removed as dead schema … re-add it THEN, with an instance'). (c) `Process`/`ProcessStep` are `part def` with behaviour in strings and sequence in an Integer, and process.sysml:5-6 records that the fields were renamed BECAUSE `action` and `output` are reserved — the model reached for `action def` and was deflected by a name clash; `.engine/workflows/business.sysml:9-21` already uses the base form correctly, so the repo holds two mechanisms for an ordered procedure. (d) `port def InterfacePort` sits in schema/core while ACCEPTED D0100 states 'No ports/interfaces enter schema/core', and `#View item def ICD` advertises a computation over data that has never existed. (e) provenance (`createdBy`/`owner`/`judgedBy`) is String while `part def Actor` exists and single-valued `ref` is portable in BOTH toolchains — ~3000 attestation records a viewer cannot navigate. (f) stale claims inside frozen schema: `query.py` cited 8 times though retired at D0074, rules.sysml:5 says 14 guards against the real 25, rules.sysml:22 classes `verifyEdge` as a native typed edge when it is a marker. (g) the 17th marker `metadata def View` lives in workflows/_meta.sysml:15, not relationships.sysml, so any audit from the declared home undercounts. VERIFY: each lettered finding is independently re-measured before any change; every schema/core edit carries the §2.5 sign-off; deletions leave no tombstones; each is a separate sprint with its own gate rather than one sweeping commit."; }
        part dcSchemaLeannessSweepDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "0d8000d8-0814-60d8-9b3e-0000003000d8"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "5f149bd"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-14"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        // The single highest-leverage base-first fix: this parser gap is WHY markers looked unavoidable.
        action dcParserSequenceSupport;
        verification dcParserSequenceSupportDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d800039-0814-6039-9b3e-000000300039"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "keel-parser REJECTS `:>> feature = (a, b, c)` — multi-valued feature assignment — with 'unexpected character (`('`)', while the KERNEL ACCEPTS it (`_spike_base_algebra.py` case ref_multi_sequence, 2026-08-14). So this is OUR defect, not a SysML v2 limitation, and it is the reason a multi-valued `ref` feature was never a candidate: sysmlv2-syntax-notes.md recorded the form as kernel-confirmed without noting the rust rejection, so `ref` features looked unusable for anything with more than one target and metadata markers on dependencies looked like the only way to express a one-to-many edge. WHY THIS RANKS HIGH: `ref` features are BASE SysML v2 structure and need no marker at all, so fixing the parser converts a whole class of custom edges into base constructs — it unblocks dcProcessAssertsConstraints and reduces what the two migrations have to invent. DO: support the sequence form in keel-parser (lexer + feature-value parse), preserve the values as an ordered list, and make `keel check`/`validate` accept what the kernel accepts here. VERIFY: the exact case the kernel accepts now passes `keel check`; a single value still works unchanged; an empty sequence `()` and a trailing comma are handled deliberately (accept or reject, but decided and tested, not incidental); the indexer resolves EVERY element of a multi-valued ref, proven by a reference that would be reported unresolved if any element were dropped; no existing file changes meaning; clippy + cargo test + validate + guard + check-engine green."; }
        part dcParserSequenceSupportDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "0d800051-0814-6051-9b3e-000000300051"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "e733115"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-14"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        // The safety property that lifting the `(` restriction incidentally removed (issue101).
        action dcSequenceMultiplicityGuard;
        verification dcSequenceMultiplicityGuardDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d800052-0814-6052-9b3e-000000300052"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "Resolves issue101. Enabling the sequence form removed a property the old lex error had provided accidentally: a sequence could not be written into a SINGLE-valued attribute either. Measured on a fresh keel init project — `createdBy = (\"you\", \"ghostActorNotRegistered\")` on a `String[0..1]` attribute passes validate AND passes `guard actors`, so an unregistered actor enters silently, defeating the control that makes provenance non-forgeable (D0037/D0129). The PRECISE rule needs multiplicity metadata that neither the AST nor the registry carries, and there is no attribute-declaration validation at all — a larger gap that must close before `ref` features can carry edges. INTERIM: warning-level `sequence-multiplicity` guard reporting EVERY sequence, which is useful rather than a placeholder because the model has ZERO today, so any hit is new. AST-based, not a text scan — grepping `= (` matched 4 hits that were ALL prose discussing the form (issue099 in miniature). Warning-level because it cannot be exact and a hard guard firing on a legitimate multi-valued ref would train its author to disable it (issue081). VERIFY (measured): real model reports 0 scanned / 0 warnings; the hazard project reports the createdBy sequence with file:line; `keel version` reports 26 guards (19 hard, 7 warning-only) computed from GUARD_NAMES; clippy clean; 161 tests; validate + all 26 guards + check-engine green."; }
        part dcSequenceMultiplicityGuardDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "0d800053-0814-6053-9b3e-000000300053"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "e733115"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-14"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        // Makes skipping VISIBLE. The reader itself (capturing these constructs) is dcParserReadBase.
        action dcParserSkipVisibility;
        verification dcParserSkipVisibilityDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d800063-0814-6063-9b3e-000000300063"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "Resolves issue102. keel-parser skips what it cannot read, silently. MEASURED with the undeclared-name discriminator against validate: control `part x : NoSuchTypeAnywhere` yields 1 semantic diagnostic, while `ref e : NoSuchTypeAnywhere`, `port p : NoSuchPortDef`, `assert constraint c : NoSuchConstraint` and `connect ghostA.p to ghostB.p` ALL validate clean — invisible, not merely unresolved. Every construct D0139 converts toward is in that set. DELIVERED: `Package.skipped` (line + lead token) recorded inside `skip_item`, the single choke point all skips pass through; warning-level `parser-coverage` guard reporting counts grouped by lead token, head-ranked with the tail collapsed. VERIFY (measured): reports 165 skipped statements across 473 files, of which 52 are `:>` specialization clauses (so the type hierarchy is unread — and `:>` is D0140's derivation form), 57 are `use case` usages, 18 are `flow`; the report stays legible (the first version printed 57 singleton element names and buried the findings); warning-level, since 165 is the honest current state and hard-failing would block every commit (issue081); 27 guards (19 hard, 8 warning-only); clippy clean; 161 tests; validate + guard + check-engine green."; }
