        action dcParserReadUseCaseAndFlow;
        verification dcParserReadUseCaseAndFlowDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d800086-0814-6086-9b3e-000000300086"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "Constructs 2 and 6 of dcParserReadBase, taken together because both are statements PRESENT and unread in the model today (the remaining three — ref, assert/require constraint, ports/connect — have zero instances and are about future capability, not current blindness). USE CASE USAGES: `use case name : Type { ... }` was skipped entirely, only `use case def` handled, so all 56 carried unchecked type references and unchecked enum literals and were absent from every view. Added `Item::UseCase(UseCaseUsage)` as its own variant rather than reusing Part — a use case is not a structural part, following the precedent Verification already sets — with registry type + attribute checking and Model ingestion. FLOW: all 18 `flow from A.x to B.y` sit inside `action def` workflow bodies; added `FlowEdge`, `ActionDef.flows`, a `parse_flow` matched on the IDENTIFIER `flow` (promoting it to a keyword would change the word's behaviour everywhere for one construct's benefit), and dotted-path capture keeping the full endpoint text. VERIFY (measured): use case usage with an UNDECLARED type FAILS, with a declared type PASSES, with attributes PASSES; `attribute def` still parses (a regression check — an earlier patch of mine landed in the attribute arm by mistake and was reverted); `parser-coverage` falls 74 -> 18 -> 0, dropping by exactly 56 then exactly 18, so every statement in the model is now read; real repo + .engine clean; clippy clean; 161 tests; all 27 guards green."; }
        part dcParserReadUseCaseAndFlowDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "0d800087-0814-6087-9b3e-000000300087"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "dedc999"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-14"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action dcModelIngestWorkflows;
        verification dcModelIngestWorkflowsDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d800088-0814-6088-9b3e-000000300088"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::demo; :>> procedureText = "Resolves issue104. `Model::build` ingests `.tracking` + `.engine/{decisions,processes,views,skills,rules}` and NOT `.engine/workflows`, so the six workflow definitions — the one place this repo models behaviour in base SysML v2 rather than in strings — contribute nothing to any view, trace, diagram or guard, and the newly-parsed flow edges have nowhere to land. DO: add the directory, then reconcile the blast radius, which is the actual work: workflow actions become items subject to charter, requirement-rootedness and critique-coverage, so guard output must be compared item by item BEFORE and AFTER and every new violation individually judged as either a real gap to fix or a scope error to exclude. Do NOT resolve it by grandfathering wholesale — that would hide precisely the traceability gaps the ingestion exists to reveal. VERIFY: flow edges appear in `keel diagram` and in a declared view; workflow actions are reachable by `keel trace`; the before/after guard diff is enumerated and each delta has a recorded verdict; no guard newly fails without that verdict; clippy + cargo test + validate + guard + check-engine green."; }
        part dcModelIngestWorkflowsDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "0d80012b-0814-612b-9b3e-00000030012b"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "f7f7296"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-14"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action dcParserReadBase;
        verification dcParserReadBaseDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d800065-0814-6065-9b3e-000000300065"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::demo; :>> procedureText = "The reader itself, and the true gate on every conversion in the base-first pass. issue102 made skipping VISIBLE; this makes the constructs READABLE. Visibility means a conversion fails loudly instead of silently; it does not mean the conversion is possible. DO: capture, with type/target resolution so an undeclared target FAILS, in priority order by what the pass needs — (1) `:>` specialization clauses on type definitions, 52 present and unread today, which is D0140's derivation form and therefore needed before any #DerivedFrom work; (2) `ref` members with their type, the base construct that replaces markers for one-to-many edges and the reason dcParserSequenceSupport was built; (3) `assert constraint` / `require constraint`, needed by D0139(D) for process-to-controls; (4) `port` members, `interface def` and `connect`, the up/downstream surface the human asked for in phase 1; (5) `use case` USAGES, 57 skipped entirely today; (6) `flow`, 18 present. VERIFY, per construct and not in aggregate: the undeclared-name discriminator FAILS for each newly-read construct, matching the `part x : NoSuchType` control — that is the only proof the content is actually captured rather than merely tolerated; `parser-coverage` drops by exactly the number of statements each step claims to have absorbed, reconciled per step; every existing view, guard and orient output is UNCHANGED for constructs already read, proven by capturing output before and after; the kernel still accepts every file (the rust parser must not diverge from conformance in the permissive direction again — issue097); clippy + cargo test + validate + all guards + check-engine green at every step."; }
        part dcParserReadBaseDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "0d800098-0814-6098-9b3e-000000300098"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "3df2ef0"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-14"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action dcAssumptionsView;
        verification dcAssumptionsViewDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d8000ea-0814-60ea-9b3e-0000003000ea"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "Resolves issue105. I recorded the deleted `Assumption` type as 'the one genuine loss' of D0142's sweep; the human corrected it — an accepted unverified requirement with downstream non-verification dependencies IS an assumption, so the class is COMPUTABLE and §1.1 makes it a view rather than an authored type. The behaviour the type was justified by (downstream suspicion) already exists per relationships.sysml:29-31 and has worked the whole time the type sat unused. DELIVERED: `keel assumptions` — unverified AND depended-upon, scoped to Need/SystemRequirement/SubsystemRequirement/Decision, with verification edges excluded from the dependency test since a Test DISCHARGES an assumption rather than resting on it. VERIFIED both directions: reports 73; a verified Need (n01AtomicItems) is correctly absent; every listed item has >=1 dependent; the three types represented are Decision, Need and SystemRequirement. clippy + cargo test + validate + all 28 guards + check-engine green."; }
        part dcAssumptionsViewDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "0d8000eb-0814-60eb-9b3e-0000003000eb"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "79e7b06"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-14"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        // EXCLUDED from D0142's sweep on purpose: both are migrations over INSTANCE data needing
        // D0067's gated expand/migrate/contract, and bundling either into a dead-code deletion would
        // make the deletion unreviewable and the migration unverifiable.
        action dcProcessAsBehaviour;
        verification dcProcessAsBehaviourDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d8000d9-0814-60d9-9b3e-0000003000d9"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::demo; :>> procedureText = "Panel finding (c), all three critics agreeing and the correctness adjudicator rating it High confidence. `Process` and `ProcessStep` are `part def` with behaviour in strings and sequence in an Integer: `actionText`, `producedArtifact`, `triggerCondition`, `order : Integer`. process.sysml:5-6 records WHY — `action` and `output` are RESERVED WORDS, so the author renamed the fields instead of reaching for the constructs those words belong to. The repo already models behaviour correctly elsewhere: .engine/workflows/business.sysml:9-21 is a clean `action def` with typed in/out parameters, `first..then` successions and `flow`. So there are TWO mechanisms for an ordered procedure and 93 ProcessStep instances use the weaker one. DO: retype to `action def` with `first..then` for order and typed `out` parameters plus `flow` for producedArtifact; `owner` stays an attribute. This is what makes a process connectable upstream/downstream by typed item flow rather than by prose — the phase-1 ask. VERIFY: `perform action` and `first..then` are kernel-confirmed valid; every existing process step survives with its order preserved, reconciled by control total; `keel activation` still reads the same 6 units and 14 asserts (D0141 attached asserts to these very parts, so the retype must preserve them); parser-coverage stays 0; a fresh keel init project passes its own gate; D0067 gated phases, green at each."; }
        part dcProcessAsBehaviourDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "0d80010a-0814-610a-9b3e-00000030010a"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "06e9154"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-14"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action dcActorTraceView;
        verification dcActorTraceViewDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d80010b-0814-610b-9b3e-00000030010b"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "Resolves issue106, and SUPERSEDES the shape of dcProvenanceAsRefs. The panel's complaint — provenance strings are unnavigable — is correct, but `ref createdBy : Actor` is unavailable: `.engine` carries 137 provenance values and would have to import `ProjectActors`, a per-project INSTANCE package, inverting the D0093 engine/instance boundary. `.engine` ships downstream via `keel init`, where this project's actors do not exist. DELIVERED INSTEAD: `keel actor-trace <actor>` computing authored / judged / owned from the strings — the same derive-do-not-author move the human reasoned to for Assumption. VERIFIED: wweatherholtz 643/233/0; claudeSonnet46 9/91/13. RESIDUAL, recorded not glossed: a raw viewer still sees strings, so navigability lives in keel rather than SysON; that is the price of not inverting the boundary."; }
        part dcActorTraceViewDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "0d80010c-0814-610c-9b3e-00000030010c"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "a14d564"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-14"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        // FRONTIER TOP: every base-first choice downstream depends on knowing which oracle answered.
        // Without this, the whole D0139 programme can be measured against the wrong tool — as it already
        // was once (issue097).
        action dcKernelConformanceGate;
        verification dcKernelConformanceGateDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d800038-0814-6038-9b3e-000000300038"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::demo; :>> procedureText = "Resolves issue097. `keel validate`/`check` are the ENGINE's semantic authority (D0048) — reference resolution, identity, provenance — and are NOT a SysML v2 conformance check, but nothing says so, so 'validate is green' reads as 'this is valid SysML v2'. It is not: the kernel rejects `verify X by Y` at package level while the rust parser accepts it, which is how a wrong migration target reached an accepted Decision (D0139 clause E). The human's goal is a model usable in a standard viewer (SysON), so conformance is the property that actually matters and it is currently unmeasured. DO: (a) a CONFORMANCE LANE running the kernel over the model and reporting non-conformance, as an INDICATOR or warning-level control — NOT hard-blocking, because D0132 demoted the per-file instance validator for failing correct files and forcing an all-or-nothing bypass (issue081), and repeating that would disable every other layer again; (b) a standing rule that any NEW base construct is kernel-validated before adoption, never on the rust parser alone; (c) state the oracle boundary plainly wherever `validate` is documented as the authority, so the next author cannot make my mistake. VERIFY: the lane runs kernel-side and reports a count plus the specific non-conforming constructs; a deliberately non-conformant construct that the rust parser accepts IS reported; the lane never blocks a commit; `keel validate` docs and CLAUDE.md §5 state what the rust authority does and does not check; the D0139 construct table is re-measured kernel-side and corrected; clippy + cargo test + validate + guard green."; }
        part dcKernelConformanceGateDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "0d80015c-0814-615c-9b3e-00000030015c"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "0bd61be"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-14"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        // Ranked ABOVE the remaining D0139 items because those are BLOCKED on an acceptance the human
        // could not see — fixing the surface unblocks the queue behind it.
        action dcPendingAcceptanceSurface;
        verification dcPendingAcceptanceSurfaceDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d800037-0814-6037-9b3e-000000300037"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::demo; :>> procedureText = "Resolves issue096. The console's Decisions surface is wired to the `keel decisions` governance scorecard, which filters to accepted (132 rows) and omits the 2 `proposed` decisions; the declared view `keel view decisions` shows all 139. Acceptance is the single human gate in an otherwise autonomous loop, so an oversight surface that renders everything EXCEPT what needs action inverts its purpose — and it fails silently, since nothing states that decisions are waiting. DO: surface PENDING ACCEPTANCES as a first-class computed count plus names in BOTH `keel orient` and the console, sourced from the declared view rather than the scorecard; leave the scorecard's accepted-only scope alone, since scoring citations and critique coverage is meaningful only for a committed decision. VERIFY: with 2 proposed decisions present, orient reports exactly those 2 by name and the console shows them; with none, both report zero rather than omitting the field (absence must be stated, not implied — the D0138 lesson); the scorecard's 132-row output is unchanged; a decision moving proposed -> accepted leaves the pending list without any other edit; clippy + cargo test + validate + guard green."; }
        part dcPendingAcceptanceSurfaceDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "0d80017b-0814-617b-9b3e-00000030017b"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "4ba45c8"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-15"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        // Human-raised direction, 2026-08-14: "minimum custom connections, maximum using real
        // relationships defined in base sysmlv2. anytime we use something custom, it should be strongly
        // critiqued." Ranked top — it governs how everything else is authored. All four AWAIT
        // acceptance of D0139 (frozen edge algebra + a 613-use migration).
        action dcBaseFirstVocabulary;
        verification dcBaseFirstVocabularyDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d800033-0814-6033-9b3e-000000300033"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::demo; :>> procedureText = "Resolves issue095 — the RULE and its control, not the migrations. Base SysML v2 relationships that parse clean in the rust authority are going unused while custom markers cover the same ground: `verify` 0 uses vs #Verify 613, `refine` 0 vs #DerivedFrom 91, plus `assert constraint`/`require constraint`/`perform action`/`include use case` all unused. The recorded justification for markers covered only genuinely NEW edge kinds (no `dependency def`) and was never re-tested. DO: (a) state the BASE-FIRST rule — a custom `metadata def` is a last resort, permitted only with a recorded justification naming the base constructs considered and why each fails; (b) an automated control (not a reminder, D0047) requiring every PROJECT-declared marker to carry that justification as a `doc` clause, warning-level for the engine's own 17 grandfathered markers (forward-only, issue068) and applying to anything new; (c) correct sysmlv2-syntax-notes.md to list the verified base relationships FIRST so the next author reaches for them before inventing an edge. VERIFY: a new undocumented `metadata def` is flagged; one carrying a justification passes; the engine's existing markers are grandfathered to warnings and the list cannot be extended; the notes name every base construct actually verified against `keel check`; clippy + cargo test + validate + guard green."; }
