        action dcProcessAssertsConstraints;
        verification dcProcessAssertsConstraintsDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d800034-0814-6034-9b3e-000000300034"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::demo; :>> procedureText = "The first application of dcBaseFirstVocabulary, and a correction of my own sprint-265 choice. Process-to-controls went into `.engine/contracts/process-units.toml` when `assert constraint` was available and is semantically exact: CLAUDE.md §1.7 already holds that the guards ARE the constraint layer, so a Process asserting the constraints it enforces is base SysML v2 stating precisely what is meant, and it makes the mapping TRACEABLE — a TOML table cannot be reached by `keel trace`, `view`, or any declared viewpoint. DO: model each guard as a `constraint` in the engine schema and each Process as asserting the ones it brings; read the activation unit mapping from the MODEL rather than from process-units.toml; retire that file with no tombstone. NOTE THE PARSER CONSTRAINT: the multi-valued `:>> x = (a, b, c)` form is rejected by the rust authority (issue095), so either several single-valued asserts are used or keel-parser gains sequence support first — decide with measurement, not preference. VERIFY: the unit mapping is computed from the model and `keel activation` output is unchanged; the mapping is reachable by a declared view; process-units.toml is gone; a fresh `keel init` project still passes with and without a manifest; the D0138 core-vs-process-bound split is preserved exactly, including the adversarial `active = []` case still hard-failing on a duplicate id; clippy + cargo test + validate + guard green."; }
        part dcProcessAssertsConstraintsDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "0d8000a8-0814-60a8-9b3e-0000003000a8"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "5b1aea4"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-14"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action dcMigrateVerifyToBase;
        verification dcMigrateVerifyToBaseDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d800035-0814-6035-9b3e-000000300035"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::demo; :>> procedureText = "Migrate `#Verify dependency from T to R` to the base relationship `verify R by T` — 613 occurrences, so this follows the D0067 migration process strictly: gated expand/migrate/contract, a COMMITTED transform, a dry run reconciling control totals, green at every step, and never fabricated history. TARGET CORRECTED 2026-08-14 (issue097): `verify X by Y` is NOT a valid target — the KERNEL rejects it and every other standalone verify form, its own message being 'A requirement verification must be in the objective of a verification case'. My earlier claim that it parsed clean measured the PERMISSIVE rust parser, not conformance. The ONLY kernel-valid verification shape is structural: `verification def V { subject s : P; objective r; }`. That makes this a REMODELLING, not a relationship swap — every Test becomes a verification case with a subject and an objective — so re-estimate before starting and treat the old estimate as void. WHY IT MATTERS BEYOND tidiness: `#Verify` is load-bearing for the hard `requirement-rootedness` and warning-level `verification-trace` guards, and a MISSPELLED marker validates clean while silently removing an item from a guard's view (the D0133 argument). A base relationship is parsed as a relationship, so the misspelling class disappears rather than being guarded against. VERIFY: control totals reconcile exactly — the count of #Verify edges before equals the count of `verify` relationships after, per file; every guard consuming verification sees the same element set before and after, proven by capturing guard output on both sides; `keel coverage`/`tier-satisfaction`/`assured` are unchanged; the expand phase leaves both forms readable so no commit is ever red; clippy + cargo test + validate + guard green at every phase."; }
        action dcMigrateDerivedFromToBase;
        verification dcMigrateDerivedFromToBaseDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d800036-0814-6036-9b3e-000000300036"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::demo; :>> procedureText = "Migrate `#DerivedFrom` (91 occurrences) to base derivation. TARGET RESOLVED 2026-08-14 by kernel measurement (issue097), so the 'decide first' step is DONE: `refine X by Y`, `trace X to Y` and `derive X from Y` are ALL REJECTED by the kernel — they are v1 keywords absent from the v2 pilot grammar — while `requirement b :> a` (specialization) is accepted. The target is therefore `:>`, exactly as sysmlv2-syntax-notes.md:16 recorded all along. My earlier report that `refine` parsed clean measured the PERMISSIVE rust parser and was wrong. #DerivedFrom is consumed by the HARD requirement-rootedness guard, so the same misspelling-blindness argument as dcMigrateVerifyToBase applies. D0067 migration process, control totals reconciled. VERIFY: the chosen target is justified in a recorded Decision against the alternative; requirement-rootedness sees the identical #Capability element set before and after; rootedness and tier-satisfaction outputs unchanged; green at every phase."; }
        // BLOCKED ON A CONTENT GATE, not on effort: editing a process definition is a CHANGE (§3a) and
        // cannot be self-accepted. Ranked above dcActivationManifest because it resolves a High-severity
        // process defect (issue092) and is a one-step edit once accepted.
        action dcReleaseAssetVerification;
        verification dcReleaseAssetVerificationDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d7f0011-0814-5f11-9b3e-0000002f0011"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::inspect; :>> procedureText = "Resolves issue092. `relTagPublish` (.engine/processes/deploy.sysml, D0135) requires verifying that release assets are ATTACHED, which is satisfied by a green workflow plus a non-empty asset list — so v0.2.0, v0.2.1 and v0.2.2 were each verified by confirming files existed at a URL, and no published asset was ever downloaded or executed. Claims about those releases' behaviour were measured on a LOCAL build and attributed to the published one. DO: extend `relTagPublish` to require obtaining a published asset and confirming it self-identifies as the tagged version and commit (`keel version`, available as of v0.2.3) before a release counts as published. AWAITS HUMAN ACCEPTANCE: this edits a process definition, which is a CHANGE (§3a) and cannot be self-accepted — the item is blocked on that gate, not on effort. VERIFY once accepted: the step names the check and the artifact it produces; the release skill deploying the process performs it; a deliberately mismatched asset (a tag whose published binary reports a different version or commit) is REJECTED rather than recorded as published; the v0.2.3 evidence (asset reports 0.2.3 / b006f10, matching the tag) is what the step would have required; doc-sync across the release surface; validate + guard + check-engine green."; }
        part dcReleaseAssetVerificationDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "0d800012-0814-6012-9b3e-000000300012"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "95ec70e"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-14"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        // The PRINCIPLED answer to what a downstream project adopts (human, 2026-08-14: "the answer
        // should be through defining portable processes, no?"). Supersedes presence-inference.
        action dcActivationManifest;
        verification dcActivationManifestDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d7c0001-0814-5c01-9b3e-0000002c0001"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::demo; :>> procedureText = "Delivers srPortActivationManifest (nProcessPortability, D0128). WHY NOW: issues 089 and 090 were both the same question wearing different clothes — WHAT HAS THIS PROJECT ADOPTED? Both fixes answer it by INFERENCE from file presence (a marker declared somewhere; a rule file existing), which is implicit and accidental: a project cannot state what it adopted, cannot see what it has not, cannot adopt a control while temporarily disabling it, and cannot take a process as a UNIT — and enforcement changes silently when a file moves. DO: a per-project ACTIVATION MANIFEST declaring which processes are active (follow the D0097 `.engine/contracts/critique-policy.toml` precedent — a declared, downstream-overridable policy file, so the pattern already exists); `keel guard` and `orient` enforce and report exactly the active set; a process activates as a UNIT (definition + deploying skill + its declared rules/guards), so activating it turns on its enforcement rather than merely adding a skill (D0059). Absent manifest => everything present is active, so no existing project changes behaviour. This SUBSUMES both patches: 'rule absent -> not adopted' becomes 'that process is not in your active set', which is a DECLARED fact rather than an inference. VERIFY: a fresh `keel init` project passes with and without a manifest; a project that activates a subset has ONLY those controls enforced and the inactive ones REPORTED (never silently dropped); activating a process turns its rules on in one step; deactivating is visible in `keel guard` output; the guard set reported by orient matches the manifest; clippy + cargo test + keel validate + guard green."; }
        part dcActivationManifestDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "0d800023-0814-6023-9b3e-000000300023"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "da8b8de"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-14"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        // Ranked BELOW dcActivationManifest: Medium severity against a p0 direction item, and the
        // friction it removes is mine, not a downstream project's.
        action dcShellAdaptationGate;
        verification dcShellAdaptationGateDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d800022-0814-6022-9b3e-000000300022"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::demo; :>> procedureText = "Resolves issue094. Host/shell adaptation errors recurred THREE times in one sitting (two MSYS paths handed to Windows Python — one of which I briefly reported to the human as a product defect before checking my own path form — and one PowerShell `&` call operator inside a bash command), the class of the closed issue065 and named in CLAUDE.md §6 as the #1 avoidable-friction class. Sprint 263's retro wrongly concluded the class was not automatable because it 'lives in throwaway shell commands, not committed artifacts'; that assumed a control must inspect ARTIFACTS, while D0134's in-loop gates inspect TOOL CALLS, and a shell command is a tool call. DO: a PreToolUse check on Bash commands (`keel hook pre-bash`) detecting (a) an MSYS-style `/c/...`, `/tmp/...` or `/mnt/...` path passed as an argument to a Windows executable, and (b) PowerShell-only syntax inside a bash command — a leading `&` call operator, `$env:`, backtick line continuation — emitting an ADVISORY message naming the concrete fix (`cygpath -w`, or switch to the PowerShell tool). ADVISORY not blocking, deliberately: this check cannot be exact, and a heuristic that blocks every shell command is the issue076/issue081 dynamic where an over-strict gate trains its actor to disable it. VERIFY: each of the three real commands from this sitting is detected and names the right remedy; a correct bash command with a genuine POSIX path is NOT flagged; a Windows path passed to a Windows program is not flagged; the hook is silent on the common case so it costs nothing; absent binary degrades to no-op without erroring (the issue093 lesson); clippy + cargo test + validate + guard green."; }
        part dcShellAdaptationGateDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "0d80018b-0814-618b-9b3e-00000030018b"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "9e14881"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-15"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action dcEnrollment;
        verification dcEnrollmentDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d5e0009-0812-4e09-9b3e-00000000e009"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::demo; :>> procedureText = "srDcEnrollment (D0129) — deploys .engine/processes/actor-enrollment.sysml. Every remote contributor starts unenrolled, and enrollment is the gate through which attribution, acceptance authority and gate uniformity are all established. Distinct from the existing `introduction` process, which onboards a fresh PROJECT (zero to first value) and never establishes WHO is acting. DO: one sanctioned command that determines the acting identity, obtains kind (human/AI) and role EXPLICITLY, registers the actor if absent without duplicating an entry, writes the machine-local identity binding, verifies the local gate actually executes, and reports readiness or the specific remedy — requiring no other contributor present, and REFUSING rather than enrolling into indeterminate identity or a non-running gate. Supersedes capture_user.py's git-identity-only, Person-only capture. VERIFY: a fresh clone with no engine identity reaches ready in one command; an AI enrolls as kind=ai (never Person); re-enrolling an existing actor re-binds without duplicating; a machine with a broken gate is reported NOT ready with the remedy; indeterminate identity is refused, never defaulted; clippy + cargo test + validate + guard green."; }
        part dcEnrollmentDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "0d8001ab-0814-61ab-9b3e-0000003001ab"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "1b69544"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-15"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action dcBlockedFrontier;
        verification dcBlockedFrontierDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d8001ac-0814-61ac-9b3e-0000003001ac"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "Resolves issue112. The ready frontier ranked `dcWorkClaim` first while it was blocked on a proposed frozen-core Decision (D0147), so the auto-followed frontier pointed the next contributor at work that provably could not be done — a COMPUTED view asserting something untrue, which is the one failure this engine's premise cannot survive. DO: exclude from `ready` any task carrying a `#DependsOn` edge to a Decision whose status is `proposed`, as a predicate DISTINCT from supersession (retired vs waiting), and author the edge for dcWorkClaim. VERIFY: ready drops the blocked item; `outstanding` does NOT drop, because blocked work is not done; the item returns to the frontier with no further edit when the Decision is accepted OR rejected; an accepted Decision blocks nothing; conservative on a model-read error, so a failure cannot silently make everything ready again; clippy + cargo test + validate + guard green."; }
        part dcBlockedFrontierDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "0d8001ad-0814-61ad-9b3e-0000003001ad"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "21122a8"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-15"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action dcWorkClaim;
        verification dcWorkClaimDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d5e0007-0812-4e07-9b3e-00000000e007"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::demo; :>> procedureText = "srDcWorkClaim + srDcStaleClaimExpiry (D0129). The ready frontier is one global list with no owner or claim (orient.rs:33-45), so several contributors rationally select the same top item and duplication surfaces only at integration. DO: a claim is an AUTHORED fact (claiming actor, item, time, commit-claimed-against) whose liveness is COMPUTED — mutual exclusion comes from the remote accepting exactly one of two concurrent claims (git ref update is a compare-and-swap), not from a lock server; add a claim write path, actor-filtered `whats-next`, and computed stale-claim expiry from git-derived time (D0013) that releases the CLAIM but never the recorded facts. VERIFY: two clones claim the same item concurrently and exactly one wins, the loser re-syncs and selects different work with no human involved; a claim held past expiry is reported stale and its item returns to the frontier; facts recorded under an expired claim remain; orient shows who holds what; clippy + cargo test + validate + guard green."; }
        part dcWorkClaimDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "2073d6ec-e457-491f-9f54-d9bd23a48d12"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "0c9836f"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-15"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action dcLandLoop;
        verification dcLandLoopDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d5e0008-0812-4e08-9b3e-00000000e008"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::demo; :>> procedureText = "srDcGateOnMergedTree + srDcSyncAwareOrient (D0129). Two contributions that each pass the gate alone can fail together, which is why the gate must run on the tree that will LAND (the not-rocket-science rule, executed client-side — no server needed). Today nothing fetches: post-commit is fire-and-forget (.githooks/post-commit:8-9). DO: `keel sync` (fetch, report divergence, ancestry-preserving integrate, gate-parity check, then orient) and `keel land` (loop: fetch, merge trunk, run the FULL enforced gate on the merged tree, push; retry on rejection with bounded back-off); make orientation report its own divergence from the remote so every computed answer carries the sync state it was computed against. VERIFY: a green-alone/red-together pair is caught BEFORE push; a push race resolves by retry without human action; retries exhaust into an honest failure rather than a force-push; orient reports behind/ahead/diverged counts; clippy + cargo test + validate + guard green."; }
        part dcLandLoopDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "0d8001cc-0814-61cc-9b3e-0000003001cc"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "fe7f8ea"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-15"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action dcUnresolvedEvidence;
        verification dcUnresolvedEvidenceDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d5e000a-0812-4e0a-9b3e-00000000e00a"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "srDcUnresolvedEvidenceClass (D0129). Under partial synchronization — the normal state of a distributed team — a contributor cannot see anchors for evidence another has recorded but not pushed, or pushed but not yet fetched here. Today both collapse into `invalidEvidence`, which orient treats as not-done (orient.rs:38, 494-531), so finished work re-enters the ready frontier and is redone. DO: distinguish 'anchor absent from THIS clone' (unsynchronized) from 'anchor genuinely unresolvable' (invalid), never count the former as work-not-done, surface the distinction in orient, and name what to fetch to resolve it. Honest-state discipline (D0098): unverifiable-from-here is a different fact from unverified. VERIFY: an anchor present remotely but unfetched reports unsynchronized (not invalid) and is NOT counted as outstanding; a genuinely dangling anchor still reports invalid; after fetching, the unsynchronized set empties with no other change; clippy + cargo test + validate + guard green."; }
        part dcUnresolvedEvidenceDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "0d8001dc-0814-61dc-9b3e-0000003001dc"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "33aa053"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-15"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action dcContentionAdjudication;
        verification dcContentionAdjudicationDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d5e000b-0812-4e0b-9b3e-00000000e00b"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::demo; :>> procedureText = "srDcContentionAdjudication (D0129). D0108 clause 5 already MANDATES that conflicting conclusions across threads be recorded as an Issue for HUMAN adjudication with neither thread silently winning — and there is no command that records an Issue at all (`record decision` exists, `record issue` does not), so the sanctioned path has no implementation, which is the friction that guarantees non-compliance (D0054). DO: add `keel record issue` (parity with `record decision`: auto number + UUID + provenance, scaffolding the #Resolves triage the issues guard requires), plus a computed contention view (two live claims on one item; two open Decisions governing one subject; contradictory recorded judgments) feeding the human-authority queue. VERIFY: `keel record issue` produces a green-validating, guard-passing triaged Issue in one call; a synthesized contradiction appears in the contention view and in what awaits human authority; adjudication is recorded as a Decision and closes it; no contributor can resolve a contention in favour of its own conclusion; clippy + cargo test + validate + guard green."; }
        part dcContentionAdjudicationDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "0d8001ec-0814-61ec-9b3e-0000003001ec"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "c400309"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-15"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action dcAuthorityQueue;
        verification dcAuthorityQueueDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d5e000c-0812-4e0c-9b3e-00000000e00c"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::demo; :>> procedureText = "srDcHumanAuthorityQueue (D0129). Human judgment is the only resource in a mostly-AI team that cannot be scaled by adding contributors, so it must never sit on the critical path of each unit of work; and asking for judgment an automated check could make degrades review into rubber-stamping, which launders unreviewed work as reviewed (D0051: confirm only what tests cannot). Builds on the D0121 review queue + D0049 batched per-sitting review, adding the distributed dimensions. DO: a computed view of everything genuinely awaiting human authority — Decisions to accept, findings at/above the disposition threshold, contentions to adjudicate, per-sitting reviews — each with WAITING AGE and ORIGINATING CONTRIBUTOR, with visible escalation past a threshold; contributors must not block while items wait. VERIFY: the queue lists exactly the items requiring human authority across several contributors, with age + origin; an item past threshold is visibly escalated; a self-evidencing test result never appears; the queue respects governing-version so historical gates are not resurrected (issue068's lesson); clippy + cargo test + validate + guard green."; }
        part dcAuthorityQueueDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "0d8001fc-0814-61fc-9b3e-0000003001fc"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "07f000f"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-15"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action dcMechanizeOwnership;
        verification dcMechanizeOwnershipDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d5e000d-0812-4e0d-9b3e-00000000e00d"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "srDcAuthorityFromRegistry + mechanize D0108 (D0129). D0108's coordination contract (owner-of-record edits fields; non-owners only ADD items/edges or SUPERSEDE; shared regions are append-or-rebase; conflicts go to the human) is CONVENTION-ONLY: absent from the seventeen guards (guards.rs:885-886) and the eight declared rules (rules.sysml:14-132), enforced by CLAUDE.md prose plus a reminder hook — and D0047 says manual vigilance is not a control. In-place writes exist that can clobber another actor's item (write.rs:551-583 set_attr; write.rs:1029 accept_decision replacen on the first proposed status), defended only by a doc comment. DO: a git-diff-aware ownership guard (cf. the doc-sync guard's diff machinery, guards.rs:785+) failing a commit that edits fields of an item whose createdBy is another actor, additive edges and supersedes excepted; and check attestation authority against the registry (kind/role) for acceptance, disposition and adjudication, refusing rather than warning. VERIFY: a non-owner field edit fails the guard while an added edge and a supersede pass; an AI actor attempting a human-only attestation is refused; per-actor landing files reduce shared-anchor conflicts (srDcPerActorWriteTargets); clippy + cargo test + validate + guard green."; }
        part dcMechanizeOwnershipDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "0d80020c-0814-620c-9b3e-00000030020c"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "1b2fd4f"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-15"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action dcSupersededNeedGaps;
        verification dcSupersededNeedGapsDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d6f0001-0813-4f01-9b3e-0000001f0001"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "Resolves issue088. `tier-satisfaction` counts a SUPERSEDED Need as an undecomposed gap. Found 2026-08-13: after decomposing the three D0128 Needs, 5 of the 6 remaining Need gaps (nViewerAuditProvenance, nViewerCoverageGap, nViewerMultiStakeholder, nViewerNotifications, nViewerTemporalState) carry a #Supersede edge from d0115/d0116 — they were DELIBERATELY DESCOPED, and §2.4 makes a superseding Decision the engine's own mechanism for scope. Demanding decomposition of descoped work understates completeness (real decomposition is 47/48 = 98%, reported as 47/53 = 88%) and, worse, invites a future contributor to 'fix' the gap by authoring SystemRequirements for work that was explicitly cut — the metric actively pointing at wrong work. DO: exclude Needs carrying an incoming #Supersede edge from the Need tier's denominator and gap set in compute_tier_satisfaction, and report the superseded count separately so the descoping stays VISIBLE rather than silently vanishing (an excluded item must not become an invisible one). Check whether the same blind spot affects rootedness/coverage/assured, which may share the assumption. VERIFY: the 5 superseded Needs leave the gap set; nViewerPersistedViewpoints (genuinely undecomposed, no supersede edge) REMAINS a gap; the superseded count is reported; a unit test covers superseded-excluded and unsuperseded-retained; clippy + cargo test + keel validate + guard green."; }
        part dcSupersededNeedGapsDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "0d80021c-0814-621c-9b3e-00000030021c"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "cc9acb3"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-15"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action dcBackfillVerifyEdges;
        verification dcBackfillVerifyEdgesDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d670001-0813-4701-9b3e-000000170001"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::inspect; :>> procedureText = "Resolves issue087. The new verification-trace guard (dcVerificationTrace, D0130/issue082) found 60 PRE-EXISTING pairs after excluding phase-gate noise and after this thread closed its own six: a delivered DoD names a SystemRequirement in its procedure while no #Verify edge reaches that requirement, so `tier-satisfaction` reports the requirement unverified even though its work is done and passing. Almost all are the keel-viewer programme (srViewer*, d0114-d0127 era). NOT fixed by the thread that built the guard, deliberately: authoring `#Verify dependency from <viewerXDoD> to <srViewerX>` asserts that that DoD's evidence actually discharges that requirement, which is a judgment only the delivering thread has the evidence to make (D0108 — add, never speak for another thread's work). DO: walk the 60 pairs; for each, either author the #Verify edge (evidence genuinely discharges the requirement) or record why it does not (the DoD delivered only part of it -> the requirement stays honestly unverified, and the prose mention should be narrowed so it stops reading as a claim). Work in reviewable batches, not one sweep. VERIFY: `keel guard verification-trace` reports 0 warnings; every #Verify edge added is defensible from its DoD's recorded evidence; sr_verified_pct rises with the METHOD MIX shown (keel tier-satisfaction verifiedByMethod) so the rise is not mistaken for functional-test coverage; keel validate + guard green."; }
        part dcBackfillVerifyEdgesDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "0d80023e-0814-623e-9b3e-00000030023e"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "6b4515d"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-15"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
        action dcRepairDuplicateIds;
        verification dcRepairDuplicateIdsDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d5e000f-0812-4e0f-9b3e-00000000e00f"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "Resolves issue080. The duplicate-identity guard (dcDuplicateIdentityGuard) found 18 distinct element ids reused across 26 locations in .tracking — bootstrap ids copy-pasted between files; `a1b2c3d4-e5f6-4a7b-8c9d-0e1f2a3b4c5d` appears FOUR times. Immutable id is the invariant that lets items share a title without colliding (§2.3), so a duplicated id makes those records not distinctly addressable and any id-keyed view or edge may resolve to the wrong element. Currently GRANDFATHERED to warning (forward-only, per the issue068 lesson) via GRANDFATHERED_DUPLICATE_IDS in keel-cli/src/guards.rs. This is a MIGRATION (D0067 — run the migration skill), NOT a guard change: mint fresh UUIDs for the later occurrences, repoint every reference, reconcile control totals (count of records before == after; no record loses provenance), keep green at each step, and never fabricate historical data. VERIFY: the grandfather list is EMPTY and removed; `keel guard duplicate-identity` passes with 0 warnings and 0 violations; record counts reconcile exactly; every re-identified record retains its original createdAt/createdBy/judgedAt provenance; clippy + cargo test + keel validate + guard green."; }
        part dcRepairDuplicateIdsDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "0d80022c-0814-622c-9b3e-00000030022c"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "33090db"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-15"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
