        action dcOneGateDefinition;
        verification dcOneGateDefinitionDoD : Test { :>> id = "e3c161db-5391-4b10-b4d3-13472150e31b"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "Resolves issue282. There are three bodies of the gate with different bars: the workspace per-project gate runs validate plus guards plus declared rules, the scaffolded hook runs validate plus guard plus rules enforce, and the sync gate runs validate plus guards and NO rules - confirmed, that file contains zero occurrences of rules. The declared-rule layer is the one a downstream project uses to add a blocking control without writing Rust, so such a project has it enforced at commit and unenforced on the merged tree, which is exactly the class the sync gate exists to catch. Its own doc comment still claims it is deliberately the SAME entry point as the commit hook. DO: make the per-project gate public, have the sync gate and the hook path use it, delete the inlined copies. VERIFY: one definition and three callers; a declared-rule violation fails all three; the doc comment is true."; }
